window.OSAP_FT=window.OSAP_FT||{};window.OSAP_FT["3e"]={"https://theconversation.com/view-from-the-hill-albanese-says-he-wants-chalmers-there-for-the-long-haul-296268":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:16Z","sha":"4a79ed8676fb3337ede6ddda989a2b73fdc27bb171a98719ff105ad91f5990d7","read":"https://theconversation.com/view-from-the-hill-albanese-says-he-wants-chalmers-there-for-the-long-haul-296268","bytes":173739,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"View from The Hill: Albanese says he wants Chalmers there for the long haul","by":[{"n":"Michelle Grattan","r":"Professorial Fellow, University of Canberra"}],"pub":"2026-11-10T08:32:37Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["In politics, rumour feeds on rumour, and the line between rumour and reality can become increasingly blurred. At the extreme, rumour can in itself help change reality."]},{"k":"p","s":["Treasurer Jim Chalmers, struggling with a mass of economic problems, is presently the centre of the Canberra rumour mill – about his relationship with the prime minister, about colleagues’ allegedly undermining him, even about suggestions he could be shifted out of the Treasury portfolio."]},{"k":"p","s":["Just before he jetted off on Tuesday to the COP climate conference in Turkey, Albanese was asked at a news conference: “Is it your preference that Jim Chalmers remains treasurer through to the next election?”"]},{"k":"p","s":["His answer was a flat “yes”."]},{"k":"p","s":["In another question he was asked: “How concerned are you about the apparent undermining of your treasurer from within government?”"]},{"k":"p","s":["He replied: “The treasurer’s doing a great job. This is a treasurer [who] has produced a couple of budget surpluses, lower deficits, that’s producing economic reform, and we’re continuing to work through with the mid-year economic forecast just in – it’ll come in next month. We’re continuing to work that through. I work closely with the treasurer.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["The answers were less important than the questions. As we know from various fronts, Albanese – who has the sole say over who is his treasurer – can always have a “change of position”."]},{"k":"p","s":["But that the questions were asked reflected that the performance and future of Chalmers, who is under fierce public criticism in sections of the press and seeming private attack from leakers in Labor, has become the object of something of a media feeding frenzy."]},{"k":"p","s":["Once this happens, ministers and other colleagues inevitably find themselves confronted by questions on the matter – and it’s easy to stumble."]},{"k":"p","s":["Thus Environment Minister Murray Watt was asked this week whether he’d like to see an end to the undermining and leaking against Chalmers."]},{"k":"p","s":["He replied: “Yes, I would” ."]},{"k":"p","s":["“As a member of a government, I take seriously the responsibility to work together, to support government policies, and also to get out there and support each other in what, at times, are very difficult times.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["Oops. Fine sentiment. Wrong answer. Watt had confirmed the leaking."]},{"k":"p","s":["He went further. “It’s pretty obvious that there are all sorts of people making all sorts of comments.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["Later, Watt was apparently surprised at the attention to his remarks."]},{"k":"p","s":["Meanwhile by Tuesday cabinet secretary Andrew Charlton, a favourite of the prime minister and seen as destined eventually for higher things, was resorting to the textbook answer when fending off questioning about his ambitions. “I’m very focused on my job.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["Chalmers can do little but also be very focused on his job which, if anything, is only going to get tougher in coming months. And trust Albanese won’t have one of his “changes of position”."]}],"chars":2820},"https://theconversation.com/from-ancient-asia-to-the-ai-age-has-chinas-beauty-stratagem-found-a-new-victim-in-nvidias-jensen-huang-296393":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:30Z","sha":"c49e1d5364f3718c968a3c5fbc108618f7cd8ca3a3a1b549d6b4721a5c990dec","read":"https://theconversation.com/from-ancient-asia-to-the-ai-age-has-chinas-beauty-stratagem-found-a-new-victim-in-nvidias-jensen-huang-296393","bytes":187628,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"From ancient Asia to the AI age: Has China’s ‘beauty stratagem’ found a new victim in Nvidia’s Jensen Huang?","by":[{"n":"Linggong Kong","r":"Ph.D. Candidate in Political Science, Auburn University"}],"pub":"2026-11-12T20:25:32Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Extramarital activities have ",["led to the resignation of","https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/11/technology/jensen-huang-nvidia-affair.html"]," one of America’s most influential tech bosses, Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang. The saga has also raised the specter of an age-old spying tactic: the “honey trap.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["Huang stepped down following ",["media reports","https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/08/technology/nvidia-investigation-jensen-huang-china.html"]," of an extramarital relationship with Alice Huang, a Singapore-based executive ",["suspected of having ties to Chinese intelligence agencies","https://thehill.com/policy/international/6212857-alice-huang-spy-allegations-nvidia-ceo-relationship/"],". She previously worked for Megaspeed, a cloud computing company ",["under U.S. scrutiny","https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/12/nvidia-megaspeed-alice-huang-jensen-.html"]," for allegedly helping China obtain restricted AI chips."]},{"k":"p","s":["Jensen Huang ",["acknowledged an affair","https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/a-message-to-our-company"],". And amid reports of a second ",["with a company executive who reports to him","https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/11/technology/jensen-huang-nvidia-affair.html"],", he tendered his resignation to the Nvidia board on Nov. 12, 2026."]},{"k":"p","s":["The sensitivity of Huang’s work – AI is increasingly seen as ",["central to national security","https://www.belfercenter.org/publication/artificial-intelligence-and-national-security"]," – has turned a company matter into an international issue. China’s Foreign Ministry has ",["denied the espionage allegations","https://x.com/CGTNEurope/status/2119783595040702490"]," and ",["accused the U.S.","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/12/china-says-us-fabricating-nvidia-spy-claims-to-justify-tech-blockade"]," of “fabricating so-called spy narratives and ⁠⁠⁠⁠using them as a pretext for imposing ⁠⁠⁠⁠a technological blockade.” Nvidia says ",["it has no reason to believe","https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/statement-from-nvidia-board-of-directors"]," company secrets have been leaked."]},{"k":"p","s":["Yet part of what makes the accusations so believable is ",["history is dotted","https://engelsbergideas.com/notebook/a-short-history-of-the-honey-trap/"]," with examples of men being distracted, compromised or spied on by attractive men and women deployed for that purpose."]},{"k":"p","s":["Indeed, in China it recalls an ancient political tactic known as “meiren ji” or the “ ",["beauty stratagem","https://doi.org/10.17265/2159-5542/2025.05.001"],".”"]},{"k":"p","s":["As a ",["scholar specializing in U.S.-China relations","https://scholar.google.com/citations?hl=zh-CN&user=SfkpAykAAAAJ"],", I believe that regardless of the particulars of the Huang case, the political logic behind the beauty stratagem – using personal relationships to obtain information, influence decisions and weaken opponents – remains relevant today. As such, it would be no surprise if the tactic was being used amid the growing U.S.-China competition over AI and advanced semiconductors."]},{"k":"h","s":["The beauty stratagem of Ancient China"]},{"k":"p","s":["“Meiren ji” is one of “ ",["Thirty-Six Stratagems","https://www.forbes.com/sites/daviatemin/2017/01/02/ancient-wisdom-for-the-new-year-the-36-chinese-stratagems-for-psychological-warfare-in-business-politics-war/"],",” a Chinese military text of uncertain origin but generally believed to have derived from oral and written histories from the Warring States period — 475–221 BCE. Its 31st stratagem states that personal relationships can be used to influence political opponents and recommends exploiting an adversary’s personal desires and emotional vulnerabilities to weaken their judgment and undermine their leadership."]},{"k":"p","s":["The “Thirty-Six Stratagems” is both a playbook for warfare and a reflection on past experience."]},{"k":"p","s":["Indeed, one of China’s most famous beauty stratagem stories dates back more than 2,000 years to the ",["Spring and Autumn period","https://www.britannica.com/event/Spring-and-Autumn-Period"]," of Chinese history."]},{"k":"p","s":["In the fifth century BCE, King Goujian of Yue, in modern-day southern China, was defeated by King Fuchai of Wu, its northern neighbor, and forced to submit. But Goujian secretly rebuilt his strength and prepared for revenge."]},{"k":"p","s":["According to the ancient historical text “ ",["Wu Yue Chun Qiu","https://cornellpress.cornell.edu/book/9781501754340/spring-and-autumn-annals-of-wu-and-yue/#bookTabs=1"],",” Yue sent two beautiful women – Xi Shi and Zheng Dan – to Fuchai, hoping he would become distracted by pleasure and neglect state affairs."]},{"k":"p","s":["The plan reportedly worked. Fuchai, seemingly enchanted by the beautiful women, gradually ignored Yue’s threat. Meanwhile, Goujian prepared for war and in 473 BCE Yue conquered Wu."]},{"k":"p","s":["Xi Shi’s actual role in Wu’s downfall remains unclear. Wu’s defeat ",["involved poor political decisions","https://www.chiculture.org.hk/sc/china-five-thousand-years/3946?"]," and Yue’s military recovery."]},{"k":"p","s":["Still, the story illustrated an important idea in Chinese political strategy: exploiting an opponent’s personal weaknesses can sometimes be more effective than confronting him directly."]},{"k":"p","s":["A similar tactic appears in the 14th-century novel “ ",["Romance of the Three Kingdoms","https://threekingdoms.com/"],",” considered one of the classics of Chinese literature."]},{"k":"p","s":["It tells the story of Dong Zhuo, a powerful warlord who controlled the imperial court during the late Eastern Han Dynasty. To remove him, Wang Yun, a senior court official loyal to the Han Dynasty promised a beautiful young woman, named Diaochan, in marriage to Dong Zhuo’s military commander. He then offered the same woman to Dong Zhuo."]},{"k":"p","s":["The scheme fueled jealousy and distrust between the two men, eventually enabling Wang Yun to persuade Dong Zhuo’s underling to kill him. Wang Yun then briefly took charge of the imperial court on behalf of the Han emperor."]},{"k":"h","s":["The ‘honey trap’ of modern spyware"]},{"k":"p","s":["In modern intelligence operations, using romantic or sexual relationships to approach targets, obtain sensitive information or exert influence is commonly called a “ ",["honey trap","https://thecsrjournal.in/honey-trapping-spy-tactic-threatening-national-security"],".” It is, moreover, certainly not unique to China. Western and Soviet intelligence agencies used intimate relationships for espionage frequently ",["during the Cold War","https://foreignpolicy.com/2010/03/12/the-history-of-the-honey-trap/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Modern China also has documented cases. One example involves French diplomat Bernard Boursicot and the ",["Chinese opera performer Shi Pei Pu","https://content.time.com/time/specials/packages/article/0,28804,1946375_1946448_1948269,00.html"],". The pair met in Beijing in 1964 and maintained a long-term intimate relationship. Throughout, Boursicot was providing French diplomatic documents to China. The spying was eventually exposed and both involved were prosecuted for espionage in France in the 1980s."]},{"k":"p","s":["A more recent example involves ",["former U.S. Rep. Eric Swalwell and Christine Fang","https://abcnews4.com/news/nation-world/swalwell-and-fang-fang-declassified-fbi-files-reveal-physical-relationship-chinese-intelligence-congressional-office"],", a suspected Chinese intelligence operative. Swalwell acknowledged in 2015 that he had been physically intimate with Fang on several occasions. At the time, Fang helped raise funds for Swalwell’s campaign and helped place an intern in his congressional office."]},{"k":"h","s":["Why Jensen Huang is strategically important"]},{"k":"p","s":["Jensen Huang’s position would make him a prime target for such a “honey trap” operation. His company has become central to the technological competition between the U.S and China. As AI becomes increasingly important to economic development, military capabilities and national security, moreover, Washington has ",["repeatedly tightened restrictions","https://www.csis.org/analysis/insight-us-semiconductor-export-controls-update"]," on advanced AI chip exports to China, hoping to slow its access to computing power."]},{"k":"p","s":["Beijing is ",["investing heavily in domestic semiconductor development","https://www.reuters.com/technology/china-sets-up-475-bln-state-fund-boost-semiconductor-industry-2024-05-27/"],". Yet Chinese companies still ",["need advanced chips","https://www.cfr.org/articles/chinas-ai-chip-deficit-why-huawei-cant-catch-nvidia-and-us-export-controls-should-remain"]," to train AI models and build data centers."]},{"k":"p","s":["From ",["Beijing’s perspective","https://app.www.gov.cn/govdata/gov/201707/20/408540/article.html"],", advanced AI chips matter not only for Chinese technology companies but for future military and technological competition."]},{"k":"p","s":["This puts Nvidia in a particularly important position and helps explain the national security concerns surrounding Huang."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, strategic motives do not prove an intelligence operation occurred. Jensen Huang’s importance does not, in itself, establish that he was targeted by a honey trap or that Alice Huang acted for Chinese intelligence. Those are allegations that still require full investigation."]},{"k":"h","s":["Espionage in the age of AI"]},{"k":"p","s":["Yet it is surely true that in modern politics – just as in ancient China’s rivalries – rivals seek information about opponents, try to influence important decisions and exploit weaknesses to gain strategic advantages."]},{"k":"p","s":["What has changed is who controls important strategic resources."]},{"k":"p","s":["In ancient China, influencing a ruler through personal relationships could affect a country’s political and military decisions. Today, approaching a major technology executive could offer access to sensitive technologies."]},{"k":"p","s":["More than 2,000 years ago, a powerful state could lose its advantage through a ruler’s personal weaknesses. Today, political and technological power are distributed differently."]},{"k":"p","s":["But as long as important decisions depend on individuals, personal relationships may remain a weakness that rivals can exploit."]}],"chars":7314},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/china":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T16:30Z","sha":"c39c91b45d09b24e36ba17ccdd7da5d94e1b5a36cd15e3363e7ce69d0363a523","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/china","bytes":87686,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"China travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-02-25T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-08-27T23:02:55+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["This travel advice covers mainland China. See travel advice for the Special Administrative Regions, ",["Hong Kong","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/hong-kong"]," and ",["Macao","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/macao"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Flash floods along the Nepal-China border"]},{"k":"p","s":["On 26 August 2026, heavy flooding and landslides have caused widespread damage in Gyirong County in Shigatse, Tibet, near the Nepal border. Buildings, roads, other transport and communications infrastructure have been severely damaged."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Chinese authorities and emergency services are responding. The authorities have warned of the risk of further flooding. The situation may change rapidly. Stay away from affected areas where possible. If you are travelling in the region, follow the advice of the Chinese government and local authorities and be alert to the changing situation."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are affected and need assistance from the UK government, call:"]},{"k":"p","s":["From China: +86 (0) 10 5192 4000"]},{"k":"p","s":["From the UK: +44 (0)20 7008 5000"]},{"k":"h","s":["Typhoons"]},{"k":"p","s":["The typhoon season in China normally runs from May to November, affecting southern and eastern coastal regions. Air travel and other forms of transport can be affected. Monitor the progress of approaching storms on the Japan Meteorological Agency and the China Meteorological Administration websites."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["guidance on tropical cyclones","https://www.gov.uk/tropical-cyclones"]," for advice about what to do if you are caught in a typhoon."]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/china/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of China’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in China set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the Chinese Embassy in the UK."]},{"k":"p","s":["Immigration authorities may collect biometric data, including scanned fingerprints and photos, to register your entry into China."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medical screening on arrival or during your time in China"]},{"k":"p","s":["You may need to pass through medical screening on entry into China. This may be body temperature scanning, or more rarely testing such as throat or nasal swabs, blood tests or medical examinations. You may be refused entry into China if you do not comply."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter China, your passport must have an ‘expiry date’ at least 6 months after the date you arrive and 2 blank pages for visas and stamping."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you renew your passport while you are in China, contact your local exit and entry office or call the national immigration service hotline on 12367, before or immediately upon receipt."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Chinese-British dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["China does not recognise dual nationality. If you were born in China to a Chinese national parent, you will be:"]},{"k":"li","s":["considered by the Chinese authorities to have Chinese nationality"]},{"k":"li","s":["treated as a Chinese citizen, even if you used a British passport to enter China"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you enter China on a Chinese passport or identity card, the British Embassy will not be able to offer you help. If you have formally renounced Chinese citizenship, you should carry clear evidence that you have done so. See ",["guidance on nationality in China","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nationality-in-china"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re a former Chinese citizen, Chinese authorities may:"]},{"k":"li","s":["treat you as a Chinese citizen"]},{"k":"li","s":["refuse you access to UK consular services"]},{"k":"p","s":["This can happen even if you entered China on a UK or other foreign passport and you:"]},{"k":"li","s":["haven’t renounced your citizenship according to Chinese law"]},{"k":"li","s":["haven’t formally advised the Chinese authorities of your UK citizenship"]},{"k":"li","s":["continue to maintain a Chinese passport or household registration"]},{"k":"p","s":["Authorities may not allow certain categories of Chinese citizens, such as state officials, to renounce their Chinese nationality under Chinese law."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should get professional legal advice if you’re unsure of your citizenship status under Chinese law."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["Until 31 December 2026, you can enter China visa free for stays of up to 30 days for business, tourism, family or friend visits or transit."]},{"k":"p","s":["This policy applies to all holders of standard British passports whose nationality is listed as ‘British citizen.’ Holders of British Crown Dependency passports whose nationality is listed as ‘British citizen’ are eligible for this policy."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you travel to China for any other reason (this includes paid work, long-term assignments, journalism, or study) or for a stay exceeding 30 days, you must apply for a visa before your trip to China."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you have any doubt about your eligibility, verify the conditions for visa free entry with the Chinese Embassy in London."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you visit China without the appropriate visa or visa free access you will be refused entry."]},{"k":"p","s":["Holders of Emergency Travel Documents are not allowed to enter China without a visa."]},{"k":"p","s":["Visa requirements for ",["Hong Kong","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/hong-kong/entry-requirements"]," and ",["Macao","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/macao/entry-requirements"]," are different."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Applying for a visa"]},{"k":"p","s":["Unless you meet the requirement for visa-free access, you must arrange a visa before travelling to China."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re 14 to 70 years old, you must apply for a visa online and then go in person to a ",["visa for China application centre","https://www.visaforchina.cn/"],". Officials will take a scan of your fingerprints as part of your application. There are visa application centres in London, Manchester, Belfast and Edinburgh. If you’re aged 13 or under, or 71 or over, you do not have to attend in person, but someone will need to submit your application on your behalf."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Chinese Embassy has further information on visa categories and how to apply."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you visit Hong Kong from mainland China and want to return to the mainland, you need a visa that allows you to make a second entry into China."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check your visa details carefully and do not overstay your visa. The authorities carry out regular checks and may fine, detain and deport you."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you want to stay in China longer than 6 months, you may need to get a residence permit."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Teaching visas"]},{"k":"p","s":["Teachers have been detained and deported for working on the wrong visa. It is your responsibility to check you’re working on the correct visa."]},{"k":"h","s":["Working in China"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can only work in China if you have a work visa (Z visa). Tourist and business visas do not allow you to work. You must also hold a valid work permit. The local police regularly carry out checks on companies and schools. If you do not follow Chinese immigration laws, there can be serious penalties, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["imprisonment"]},{"k":"li","s":["fines"]},{"k":"li","s":["deportation"]},{"k":"li","s":["an exit ban, which stops you from leaving China"]},{"k":"li","s":["an exclusion order, which stops you from returning"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you leave the UK, contact the Chinese Embassy in the UK to check Z visa requirements. When submitting your application, and when you receive your work permit, check the details are correct, including the location you’ll be working in. If the details are incorrect – even if your employer or others submitted the application on your behalf – the authorities can detain you, fine you or deport you."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are going to change employer once you’re in China, or change location in China with an existing employer, check with the Chinese authorities whether you need a new visa and work permit before doing so."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter China, you must have a certificate to prove you’ve had a yellow fever vaccination if you’re coming from a ",["country listed as a transmission risk","https://nathnacyfzone.org.uk/factsheet/65/countries-with-risk-of-yellow-fever-transmission"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["For full details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s China guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/49/china#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Registering with the authorities"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must register your place of residence with the local Public Security Bureau within 24 hours of arrival. Chinese authorities enforce this rule with regular spot-checks of foreign nationals’ documentation. If you’re staying in a hotel, they will register you when you check in."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about goods you can take into or out of China. You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"p","s":["This guide also has safety advice for ",["regions of China","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/china/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in China"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist attacks in China cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"p","s":["Although foreigners have not been specifically targeted, attacks may happen in places visited by foreigners. Take particular care during national holidays and at public transport hubs, and always follow the advice of the local authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Risks under Chinese national security laws"]},{"k":"p","s":["Chinese national security laws have broad scope, and you can be arrested without having intended to break the law. See advice in the Laws and cultural differences section below for more information."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["China is ruled by a single political party. Though China is open to foreign visitors, be aware of political and cultural sensitivities."]},{"k":"p","s":["Avoid any demonstrations or large gatherings. The Chinese authorities enforce public order strictly, and you may face arrest, detention and deportation. Foreign journalists have been intimidated, assaulted or detained for trying to report on demonstrations."]},{"k":"p","s":["You may risk becoming a target when there is an anti-foreign feeling or attitude in China. Keep informed of developments and follow the advice of the local authorities. During periods of tension, the authorities may block:"]},{"k":"li","s":["news reporting"]},{"k":"li","s":["access to text-messaging"]},{"k":"li","s":["the internet"]},{"k":"li","s":["international telephone lines"]},{"k":"p","s":["Posting, publishing or supporting political statements critical of China or the Chinese authorities could be viewed as illegal."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Serious crime against foreign nationals is relatively rare, but incidents can happen. Take care of your belongings at major tourist sites and other busy places, particularly where foreign nationals gather."]},{"k":"p","s":["Indiscriminate attacks using vehicles as a weapon, and knife attacks, have occurred in public places including at schools, supermarkets and hospitals. Although rare, there have been isolated violent attacks that have impacted foreign nationals."]},{"k":"p","s":["Penalties for fighting and physical altercations can range from fines to short term detentions to longer-term criminal detentions, regardless of who was initially responsible. Compensation claims by injured parties are commonplace, again regardless of who was initially responsible."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drink spiking and sexual assault"]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not leave drinks unattended or accept drinks from strangers. Personal attacks and sexual assaults are rare, but they can happen, including through drinks being spiked. Women, travelling alone or with female friends, could be at higher risk."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Scams"]},{"k":"p","s":["Beware of scams in popular tourist areas. A common example is the ‘tea tasting’ or ‘massage’ scam. Friendly strangers may invite you to visit a bar, to drink tea or have a massage, and the establishment then demands a large fee. This can be followed by threats, violence or credit card fraud."]},{"k":"p","s":["Due to the prominence of QR code usage in China, travellers are recommended to familiarise themselves with the ",["risks of QR code phishing","https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/blog-post/qr-codes-whats-real-risk"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Commercial disputes"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before entering a commercial contract in China, take legal advice, both in the UK and in China. Contracts agreed in the UK are not always recognised by Chinese courts."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re involved in or connected to a business or civil dispute, the Chinese authorities may impose an exit ban, which means you cannot leave China until the matter is resolved. British nationals have been detained against their will, intimidated and forced to pay money."]},{"k":"p","s":["Violence is rare, but it can be threatened. Report any threats of violence to the Chinese police."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["Always carry your passport with you. Police carry out random checks, especially during periods of heightened security and major sporting or political events. They do not accept printed copies. Failure to produce your ID can lead to a fine or detention."]},{"k":"hs","s":["National security laws"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a risk of detention for alleged breaking of national security laws"]},{"k":"p","s":["China’s authorities have detained foreign nationals who break national security laws. Both ‘national security’ and ‘national interest’ appear regularly in Chinese legislation and have broad scope. You may be detained without having intended to break the law. Activity that happened outside of mainland China – including online activity – could fall under the scope of mainland legislation. There is a risk of arbitrary detention, including of British nationals."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re detained on grounds of national security, you may be held for up to 6 months before formal arrest and denied legal representation before charges are brought."]},{"k":"p","s":["You may be at greater risk of surveillance, harassment or detention while in China if you are currently or have previously been linked to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["foreign governments"]},{"k":"li","s":["media outlets"]},{"k":"li","s":["NGOs"]},{"k":"p","s":["This list is not exhaustive and may change over time. This is particularly relevant when travelling to areas with large ethnic minority populations, including but not limited to Xinjiang. See ",["Regional risks","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/china/regional-risks"]," for more information."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travelling to sensitive areas"]},{"k":"p","s":["All foreign nationals travelling to or around ‘sensitive’ areas might attract more attention from the authorities, including when:"]},{"k":"li","s":["taking photographs"]},{"k":"li","s":["engaging with political groups or charities"]},{"k":"li","s":["engaging with ethnic minority populations"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sensitivities can change over time and may heighten around particular dates or events."]},{"k":"p","s":["There are restricted military areas, where foreigners can be detained, fined or deported if found without prior authorisation. Signs typically indicate where these areas are, but it may not always be clear."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Religious activities and materials"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Chinese authorities formally recognise five religions: Buddhism, Catholicism, Islam, Protestantism and Taoism. Only officially registered religious organisations are permitted to carry out religious activities. Even officially registered organisations must observe restrictions on some religious activities, including preaching and distributing religious materials."]},{"k":"p","s":["Updated ",["religious legislation","https://www.sara.gov.cn/static/content/ywdt/qtyw/2025-04-01/1356550603266883584.html"]," (Chinese language only) entered into force on 1 May 2025, setting out rules that foreigners must abide by when practicing religion in China. The paragraph below summarises some of the key information included in the link, but local legal advice should be sought for full and further detail."]},{"k":"p","s":["Foreigners may participate in religious activity that complies with Chinese law in registered temples, churches, or other approved temporary locations. Foreign nationals may invite Chinese religious professionals to hold baptisms, weddings, funerals, dojos, pujas, and other religious ceremonies for them. Foreigners wanting to hold religious activities or events in approved locations must meet the specific requirements and use the correct application procedures. One of these requirements (Article 10) is that Chinese religious professionals should preside over religious activity. Another (Article 16), is that group religious activities held by foreigners in China are limited to participation by foreigners in China. Collective religious events involving Chinese citizens are prohibited unless officially approved by the Chinese government. There are also rules for foreign religious professionals visiting China to give lectures or sermons, and those bringing in religious materials."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Falun Gong movement and others that the authorities consider as ‘cults’ are banned in China."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Criminal proceedings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Chinese laws and procedures relating to the arrest, detention and trial of criminal suspects are different from in the UK. For example:"]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re detained, only your legal representatives and consular staff from the British Embassy are allowed to visit you until your sentence is passed"]},{"k":"li","s":["there is no trial by jury, the presiding judge or judges will reach a verdict and then pass sentence if they find the accused guilty"]},{"k":"li","s":["if a case goes to trial, the conviction rate is 99%"]},{"k":"li","s":["if an offence does not meet the threshold for criminal proceedings, the police still have the right to sentence someone for up to 15 days administrative detention without trial"]},{"k":"p","s":["The granting of bail is rare, and the criminal process can often last at least a year before a verdict is passed. If bail is granted the accused will not be permitted to leave China."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re suspected of a crime, the Chinese authorities can stop you from leaving China by:"]},{"k":"li","s":["withholding your passport"]},{"k":"li","s":["applying an exit ban"]},{"k":"li","s":["detaining you for up to 37 days without charge"]},{"k":"p","s":["The thresholds for detention and prosecution in China differ from those in Hong Kong."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can be convicted in China of a crime committed against the Chinese State or a Chinese citizen, even if that crime was committed outside of China."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Exit bans"]},{"k":"p","s":["Foreign nationals can be subject to exit bans. Exit bans can:"]},{"k":"li","s":["be put on people involved in commercial or private disputes to stop them from leaving mainland China"]},{"k":"li","s":["relate to investigations into an individual, their family or an employer"]},{"k":"li","s":["be used in criminal and civil matters, including business disputes"]},{"k":"p","s":["An exit ban can last for months or years or until a legal process is complete. You may not be aware you are subject to an exit ban until you try to leave mainland China."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Money"]},{"k":"p","s":["Cashless payments through smartphone apps such as WeChat or Alipay are almost universal."]},{"k":"p","s":["Outside major cities, credit cards are not always accepted and ATMs are limited. It is not possible to exchange Scottish or Northern Irish banknotes."]},{"k":"p","s":["Counterfeit banknotes (especially 100 renminbi notes) are common, including from ATMs. Banks will not replace them. Check notes carefully before accepting them."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs penalties and prison sentences"]},{"k":"p","s":["Possession, trafficking and manufacture of any illegal drugs, including cannabis, are serious offences in China. The Chinese authorities have a zero-tolerance policy and those caught face heavy fines, lengthy prison sentences, or the death penalty."]},{"k":"p","s":["Airports in China have security equipment and procedures in place for scanning and detecting illegal items, including cannabis. This is also used to scan all the baggage of transiting passengers."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Chinese authorities can randomly test foreign nationals for drugs, including on entry to the country. Police can also raid homes as well as nightclubs and bars. If you’re in a bar that’s raided, you will be subject to on-the-spot testing and immigration checks. This may involve:"]},{"k":"li","s":["being kept at the location, or another location, for several hours"]},{"k":"li","s":["giving hair and urine samples"]},{"k":"li","s":["passport and visa checks"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you test positive, the Chinese authorities can prosecute you regardless of where or when you took drugs. Testing positive for drugs, or being found in breach of your visa conditions, can lead to heavy fines, detention and deportation."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Internet access"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Chinese authorities control internet access. Internet access can be limited around key political events and dates. Some services are permanently blocked, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Google"]},{"k":"li","s":["Facebook"]},{"k":"li","s":["YouTube"]},{"k":"li","s":["X"]},{"k":"p","s":["Other websites may sometimes be blocked."]},{"k":"p","s":["China’s cybersecurity laws state that online products and services (for example, VPNs) need to be licensed by the Chinese government. See the Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (in Mandarin). Make sure you stay informed and follow Chinese law."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drone regulations"]},{"k":"p","s":["The import, possession and use of drones and drone components are strictly regulated. Contact the ",["Civil Aviation Administration of China","https://www.caac.gov.cn/English/"]," before travel for the latest rules regarding registration and use."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Building safety"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire protection standards in Chinese accommodation are not always the same as in the UK. You should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check fire precautions, including access to fire exits"]},{"k":"li","s":["make sure your accommodation has a working fire alarm"]},{"k":"li","s":["regularly check fire exits are not blocked"]},{"k":"p","s":["Make sure your accommodation has a working carbon monoxide alarm. People have died of carbon monoxide poisoning due to incorrectly installed gas equipment."]},{"k":"p","s":["Energy UK has ",["advice on how to stay safe and the symptoms to look out for","https://www.energy-uk.org.uk/customers/carbon-monoxide/"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Teaching in China"]},{"k":"p","s":["Thoroughly research the school or university that is hiring you in China and make sure you’re confident they are following the law."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex relationships are legal in China, but public attitudes are less tolerant than in the UK. Showing affection in public may receive negative attention. There’s no Chinese law guaranteeing freedom from discrimination on the grounds of sexual orientation."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"h","s":["Hiking"]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not hike alone in isolated areas, including on the Great Wall, due to unpredictable weather conditions and the chance of injuries. Even when hiking in a group, leave your itinerary, mobile number and expected time of return at your hotel or with a third party."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must have a Chinese driving licence and valid insurance to drive in China. There are harsh penalties for driving without these, including fines and detention."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drive with caution in China. Accidents are common due to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["the poor quality of roads"]},{"k":"li","s":["high volumes of traffic"]},{"k":"li","s":["low driving standards"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re involved in a serious traffic accident, call the police. Do not move your vehicle until they arrive but make sure you and your passengers are in a safe place. If there are injuries, you may be held legally responsible for medical costs. You will also be held responsible if you hit a pedestrian."]},{"k":"p","s":["There are harsh penalties including fines, detention and imprisonment for driving under the influence of alcohol, even at very low levels."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Rail travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must show your passport to buy a ticket and to board a train in China."]},{"k":"p","s":["Trans-Mongolian Express trains (Beijing-Moscow via Ulaanbaatar) are known for smuggling. Petty theft on overnight trains is common. Search your compartment and lock the cabin door before the train leaves."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Sea travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["Mariners should avoid the disputed territory between China and other countries in the East China Sea and the South China Sea. There have been incidents of piracy and armed robbery against ships in the area. ReCAAP Information Sharing Centre has further information about piracy and armed robbery."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taxis"]},{"k":"p","s":["Avoid travelling in unmarked or unmetered taxis, as there have been incidents of sexual assault and robbery against foreign nationals. In marked taxis, make sure someone knows where you are and try to take a note of the taxi’s number."]},{"k":"p","s":["Disputes over taxi fares can quickly escalate. Any physical altercation could lead to detention regardless of who is to blame. Insist on paying the metered fare and ask for a receipt. The receipt should have the taxi number on it."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to ",["prepare for and respond to extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Earthquakes"]},{"k":"p","s":["China is in an active seismic zone and can experience major earthquakes. Learn what to do before, during and after an earthquake from the US Federal Emergency Management Agency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Typhoons"]},{"k":"p","s":["The typhoon season in China normally runs from May to November, affecting southern and eastern coastal regions. Air travel and other forms of transport can be affected. You should monitor the progress of approaching storms on the Japan Meteorological Agency and the China Meteorological Administration websites."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["guidance on tropical cyclones","https://www.gov.uk/tropical-cyclones"]," for advice about what to do if you are caught in a typhoon."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Flooding"]},{"k":"p","s":["Flooding and storms are common between May and November. You should monitor local weather reports and follow the advice and instructions of local authorities, including any evacuation orders."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["This section has safety advice for regions of China. It only covers regions where FCDO has specific advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/china"]," and ",["safety and security advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/china/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Tibet and the Tibet Autonomous Region (TAR)"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must get a permit to travel to Tibet and the Tibet Autonomous Region (TAR) through a specialist travel agent in China. You must also travel on an organised tour. The Chinese authorities sometimes stop issuing permits without notice or restrict travel to Tibetan Autonomous Prefectures (TAPs) in neighbouring provinces, even if you have a permit. Check with tour operators or travel agents and other media for information."]},{"k":"p","s":["Once in Tibet, avoid large public gatherings. Political and ethnic tensions have historically generated violent protests. Security measures will be tight. Unauthorised gatherings may be dispersed by force. Do not film or take photographs of public security forces or any altercations."]},{"k":"p","s":["Local authorities will react negatively if you’re found carrying letters or packages from Tibetan nationals to be posted in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["You must ask before you take photographs in Buddhist monasteries."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ability of the British Embassy to help British nationals in the TAR is limited."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Xinjiang Uyghur Autonomous Region"]},{"k":"p","s":["Violent unrest in Xinjiang has resulted in deaths. There are allegations that police use lethal force to disperse protests."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Chinese authorities have a significant security presence throughout Xinjiang, both as a visible demonstration of state control and to respond quickly and harshly to any unexpected demonstrations or outbreaks of violence."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been widespread arbitrary arrests and extra-judicial detentions in Xinjiang, mainly affecting the local population, particularly Uyghurs and other ethnic minorities. You may be at increased risk if:"]},{"k":"li","s":["you’re of Turkic descent"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have lived previously in Xinjiang"]},{"k":"li","s":["your appearance or practices are perceived to be Muslim"]},{"k":"li","s":["you publicly participate in Islamic religious practices"]},{"k":"li","s":["you’re a foreign national"]},{"k":"p","s":["Expect airport-style security measures, including passport and security checks, at shopping centres, markets and parks. You may need to give the security forces your phone number, have your photograph taken and explain why you are travelling. Organisations monitoring facilities in Xinjiang are at risk of harassment and detention."]},{"k":"p","s":["Always carry your passport and avoid all protests and large crowds. Do not photograph or film protests, large crowds, security officials or installations, or anything of a military nature. Mosques and other religious sites are also considered ‘sensitive’ by the authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ability of the British Embassy to help British nationals in Xinjiang Uyghur Autonomous Region is limited."]},{"k":"hs","s":["China-Myanmar border"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is continuing violence on the Myanmar side of the China-Myanmar border, which has occasionally spilled over into China."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 120 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for China","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/49/china#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["Altitude sickness is a risk in parts of China. Read more about ",["altitude sickness on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/26/altitude-illness"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in China","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/49/china"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["avian influenza"]},{"k":"li","s":["dengue – present in Guangdong Province and some other parts of China, mainly during the rainy season from May to November"]},{"k":"p","s":["Tap water is generally not safe to drink. Only drink bottled water."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re take prescription medication, make sure you bring enough with you or have access to a supply in China. Certain medicines may not be available and you may be not allowed to bring others into the country. For more information, check with your GP and the Chinese Embassy before travelling."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in China"]},{"k":"p","s":["Healthcare is not free in China and can be very expensive. Make sure you have appropriate travel and medical insurance covering healthcare and medical evacuation and repatriation. For more information, see ",["medical treatment in China","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/medical-treatment-in-china"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a list of ",["doctors and medical facilities in China","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/list-of-hospitals-in-china"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in China"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance: 120"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire: 119"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: 110"]},{"k":"p","s":["Traffic Police: 122"]},{"k":"p","s":["Maritime Search and Rescue Centre: 12395"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=China"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=China"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=China"]," in China"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in China","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/bereavement-in-china"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in China","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/china-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis,","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"]," such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/china/british-embassy-beijing"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"p","s":["If your passport is lost or stolen, go to the nearest police station or Public Security Bureau and get a report of the incident."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/china/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in China","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-china"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":31562,"change":"\"Updated information on Warnings and insurance\""},"https://theconversation.com/england-needs-120-of-normal-rainfall-to-come-out-of-drought-this-winter-storms-wont-fix-that-yet-295121":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"070fc96555ee1d1cea195a4fc220d106b974faadd774986c8f54d99441e2a15c","read":"https://theconversation.com/england-needs-120-of-normal-rainfall-to-come-out-of-drought-this-winter-storms-wont-fix-that-yet-295121","bytes":181270,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"England needs 120% of normal rainfall to come out of drought this winter – storms won’t fix that yet","by":[{"n":"Hannah Cloke","r":"Regius Professor of Meteorology and Climate Science, University of Reading"}],"pub":"2026-10-29T12:29:56Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Storm Austen and Storm Boelo swept across the UK in quick succession. Rain lashed windows, river levels rose and the clocks went back. It feels like winter has arrived all at once."]},{"k":"p","s":["Yet most of England and ",["Wales","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/crk661074ejo"]," are still in ",["drought","https://theconversation.com/topics/drought-390"],". Parts of Cornwall, Devon, Dorset, Somerset and Sussex are in ",["severe drought","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/government-activates-plan-to-protect-consumers-as-severe-drought-declared-in-parts-of-england"],", the highest level the Environment Agency can declare. South-west and south-east England have had their driest April to September in over 100 years."]},{"k":"p","s":["But how can this be when it is cold, dark and pouring with rain? Drought tightens or loosens its grip over months, not just a few days. It will be the rainy or dry nature of the months ahead that will decide whether the drought ends, or more worryingly, if it stays into next summer."]},{"k":"h","s":["Winter should fill the tank"]},{"k":"p","s":["Summer droughts grab the headlines and public attention. When we have a dry period over the summer it’s ",["everywhere, with brown lawns, hosepipe bans and cracked reservoir beds","https://www.aol.com/articles/devastating-impact-england-drought-pictures-110348000.html"],". But winter is when the UK refills its water stores."]},{"k":"p","s":["Between October and March, with shorter and colder days, less water evaporates and plants drink far less, so rain finally has the chance to refill our reservoirs. Rain can soak deep into the ground to top up groundwater, the water held in chalk and sandstone rocks beneath our feet, like a giant sponge. Groundwater supplies much of the drinking water in southern and eastern England, but it fills slowly, over months and years."]},{"k":"p","s":["Think of it like a bank account. A hot summer is a big splurge with lots of withdrawals. But a dry winter means the deposits needed to repay that spending never arrive. That’s why a dry winter can do more damage to our water supplies than a heatwave."]},{"k":"p","s":["This year the account is badly overdrawn. Southern England has just had its ",["driest April to September","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/water-situation-national-monthly-reports-for-england-2026/water-situation-september-2026-summary#"]," in more than a century, alongside ",["record summer heat","https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/blog/2026/deep-dive-after-a-remarkable-summer-meteorological-autumn-has-arrived"],". The Wimbleball reservoir on Exmoor, which supplies around 400,000 people, was full in March, after an extremely wet winter. Then the weather shifted from wetter than normal to dryer than normal. By October, water ",["levels at Wimbleball","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6d094jgg07eo"]," were down to about 13%."]},{"k":"h","s":["How unusual is a winter drought?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Droughts that carry on into winter are rare in the UK. Usually the autumn rain arrives, soil gets wetter and water stores recover by Christmas. Every instinct suggests that a stormy autumn should fix things."]},{"k":"p","s":["In the 2010 to 2012 drought, ",["two dry winters in a row","https://nora.nerc.ac.uk/id/eprint/503643/"]," left groundwater in south and east England so low that ",["hosepipe bans","https://www.channel4.com/news/hosepipe-ban-to-begin-in-drought-hit-parts-of-uk"]," came in during spring 2012. Then the wettest April on record arrived, and parts of the country swung from drought to flood within weeks."]},{"k":"p","s":["What stands out now is how quickly droughts are stacking up. This is the ",["third drought in England in five years","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/environment-agency-declares-drought-status-in-yorkshire"],". The stores barely have time to recover before the next one begins."]},{"k":"p","s":["England and Wales can be in drought and experience flooding simultaneously. After months of dry weather it takes a lot of rain to wet the ground all the way through. Storms such as Boelo and Austen bring bursts of heavy rain. Drains are overwhelmed and rivers rise quickly, sometimes spilling out onto their floodplains. But the reservoirs and groundwater that supply our taps respond far more slowly, and need lots of water to fill up. So the type of winter rains make a big difference."]},{"k":"p","s":["Floods can hit fast. But the key parts of the water cycle are slow. Rain has to wet the soil all the way through before it can trickle down into the rock, and groundwater can take months to respond. The Environment Agency estimates England needs around ",["120% of normal rainfall","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/government-environment-agency-and-water-companies-step-up-efforts-to-prevent-england-remaining-in-drought-by-next-spring"]," between September and April for every area to come out of drought. We would need half a year with consistently more rain than we would expect at this time of year to break this drought."]},{"k":"p","s":["If rain falls short across the whole winter, then England could start next summer in drought, with even less room for manoeuvre."]},{"k":"h","s":["Will winter droughts become more common?"]},{"k":"p","s":["But climate scientists don’t expect there will be a dry winter every year, either now or in decades ahead as fossil fuel burning continues to heat the planet. Climate projections suggest ",["UK winters will get wetter","https://journals.ametsoc.org/view/journals/clim/33/17/jcliD200089.xml"]," on average. But averages can hide the danger."]},{"k":"p","s":["Summers heated by the burning of fossil fuels ",["dry out the soil","https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/jul/23/climate-crisis-heat-sucking-soils-dry-extreme-drought-europe-analysis"]," and drain reservoirs faster, leaving a bigger hole for winter to fill. Winter rain is increasingly arriving in heavy bursts that are harder to capture and more likely to flood. And natural swings in the weather will still deliver dry winters from time to time. Put those together and the chance of a drought rolling into winter, or across two winters, goes up. This is a trend that will keep getting worse as long as we keep heating the planet."]},{"k":"p","s":["Preparing starts with ",["how we think about water","https://theconversation.com/englands-water-crisis-needs-more-than-just-new-reservoirs-heres-what-will-help-257922"],". It is precious, not endless. Yet we have built a system that lets a wet winter rush away to the sea, then rations what is left through a dry summer."]},{"k":"p","s":["As with other parts of the UK’s water system, we are paying the price now for years of chronic underinvestment. Water companies need to move much faster on fixing leaks, which still ",["waste billions of litres","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/water-resources-2024-2025-analysis-of-the-water-industrys-annual-water-resources-performance/water-resources-2024-to-2025-analysis-of-the-water-industrys-annual-water-resources-performance"]," every day. New ",["reservoirs","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/government-steps-in-to-build-first-major-reservoirs-in-30-years"]," are being planned in England, which will help deliver more storage. But they will take more than a decade to build, cost billions of pounds, and cannot be the whole solution."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["England’s water crisis needs more than just new reservoirs – here’s what will help","https://theconversation.com/englands-water-crisis-needs-more-than-just-new-reservoirs-heres-what-will-help-257922"]]},{"k":"p","s":["There is also a low-tech local answer, that ",["would need regulation","https://www.ciwem.org/news/planet-possible-unlocking-uk-growth-plans-through-water-reuse"]," and a programme of retrofitting to make a big difference. Rainwater harvesting and water reuse in new and existing homes, sustainable drainage in our towns, and encouraging wetlands and floodplains that ",["hold water like a sponge","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nature-based-solutions-environment-agency-position-statement/nature-based-solutions-environment-agency-position-statement"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Changes to our landscape and homes can help prevent ",["both problems","https://www.watermagazine.co.uk/2026/06/29/too-little-water-why-drought-is-the-other-half-of-flood-resilience/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Each of us ",["can help","https://www.southwestwater.co.uk/household/your-services/save-water/come-rain-or-shine"],". Take shorter showers, ask your water company about a meter and and fit a ",["water butt to catch rain","https://www.wessexwater.co.uk/your-water/how-to-install-a-water-butt"]," for the garden. These habits add up."]},{"k":"p","s":["Autumn storms have started to replenish water reserves, but it’s not the finish line. We also need to get ahead of what is likely to happen in years to come."]}],"chars":6012},"https://theconversation.com/laki-laki-tidak-bercerita-riset-temukan-ada-beban-maskulinitas-dalam-mengelola-patah-hati-292718":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:47Z","sha":"97b3f57c2b3af24a30ffe372cb6120151e2c3b42693236985af150f6229b6e9f","read":"https://theconversation.com/laki-laki-tidak-bercerita-riset-temukan-ada-beban-maskulinitas-dalam-mengelola-patah-hati-292718","bytes":179909,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"‘Laki-laki tidak bercerita’: Riset temukan ada beban maskulinitas dalam mengelola patah hati","by":[{"n":"Moh. Faiz Maulana","r":"Lecturer in the Program Study of Sociology at the University Nahdlatul Ulama of Indonesia, Jakarta., Universitas nahdlatul ulama indonesia"}],"pub":"2026-09-29T11:07:55Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["● Laki-laki belajar menyembunyikan kerentanan karena norma maskulinitas yang menuntut ketangguhan."]},{"k":"p","s":["● Mereka tetap bercerita, tetapi melalui humor, musik, nongkrong, dan kebersamaan."]},{"k":"p","s":["● Ketangguhan tidak seharusnya mengharuskan laki-laki menyamarkan kesedihan dan kerentanannya."]},{"k":"p","s":["“Laki-laki tidak bercerita” belakangan menjadi ungkapan populer di media sosial Indonesia. Kalimat ini kerap digunakan untuk menggambarkan kecenderungan laki-laki menyimpan masalahnya sendiri, termasuk ketika menghadapi persoalan dalam hubungan asmara."]},{"k":"p","s":["Namun, apakah laki-laki benar-benar tidak bercerita?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Kajian tentang ",["maskulinitas","https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0891243205278639"]," telah lama menunjukkan bahwa laki-laki berhadapan dengan tuntutan sosial untuk terlihat kuat, rasional, mandiri, dan mampu mengendalikan emosi. ",["Di Indonesia","https://doi.org/10.1080/13639810903269318"],", tuntutan tersebut juga berkaitan dengan harga diri, kedewasaan, tanggung jawab, dan kemampuan menjaga pengakuan sosial sebagai laki-laki."]},{"k":"p","s":[["Penelitian kami","https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/10608265261490854"]," terhadap pengalaman patah hati tujuh laki-laki muda Indonesia berusia 19–30 tahun menemukan persoalan yang lebih kompleks."]},{"k":"p","s":["Mereka bukan tidak sedih atau tidak ingin bercerita. Mereka justru terus mengatur kapan, kepada siapa, dan dengan cara apa kesedihan boleh terlihat. Diamnya laki-laki bukan semata pilihan personal, melainkan hasil dari norma sosial yang mengajarkan laki-laki bahwa kerentanan perlu disembunyikan."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["Apa itu ‘toxic masculinity’ dan dari mana asalnya?","https://theconversation.com/apa-itu-toxic-masculinity-dan-dari-mana-asalnya-203558"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Patah hati, tapi harus terlihat ‘baik-baik saja’"]},{"k":"p","s":["Temuan pertama kami menunjukkan bahwa putus cinta tidak otomatis mengakhiri kesedihan. Rasa patah hati tetap membekas dalam hati laki-laki meski hidup harus terus berjalan."]},{"k":"p","s":["Para informan tetap bekerja, kuliah, menjalani aktivitas sehari-hari, dan bertemu teman. Mereka berusaha terlihat “baik-baik saja”."]},{"k":"p","s":["Sebagian memilih diam, menghindari pembicaraan tentang kehidupan pribadi, atau sengaja menyibukkan diri. Diam, dalam konteks ini, bukan berarti tidak punya emosi, melainkan jadi cara mereka menyembunyikan kesedihan."]},{"k":"p","s":["Temuan ini sejalan dengan gagasan sosiolog Amerika ",["Arlie Hochschild","https://doi.org/10.1525/9780520951853"]," bahwa kehidupan sosial memiliki aturan mengenai bagaimana emosi seharusnya dirasakan dan ditampilkan."]},{"k":"p","s":["Dalam konteks patah hati, laki-laki tidak hanya berhadapan dengan kesedihan, tetapi juga tuntutan untuk tetap terlihat mampu mengendalikan dirinya."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["Tips hadapi patah hati saat ditolak gebetan—saran dari psikolog","https://theconversation.com/tips-hadapi-patah-hati-saat-ditolak-gebetan-saran-dari-psikolog-253248"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Ketika menangis dianggap tidak cukup ‘laki-laki’"]},{"k":"p","s":["Hal itu terlihat lebih jelas ketika para informan berbicara tentang menangis."]},{"k":"p","s":["Keinginan untuk menangis sebenarnya ada. Namun, menangis dapat memunculkan kekhawatiran akan dianggap lemah, terlalu sensitif, tidak dewasa, atau tidak mampu mengendalikan diri."]},{"k":"p","s":["Bagi para informan, menangis bukan tentang respons terhadap kesedihan, melainkan sesuatu yang harus dipertimbangkan: bolehkah saya menunjukkan ini? Apa yang akan dipikirkan orang lain?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ekspektasi semacam ini bahkan dapat berubah menjadi pengawasan terhadap diri sendiri."]},{"k":"p","s":["Salah satu partisipan menggambarkan seolah ada “rem” di kepalanya ketika ingin menangis. Ia tidak selalu membutuhkan orang lain untuk mengatakan bahwa laki-laki seharusnya kuat. Aturan itu sudah tertanam dan membuatnya otomatis menahan diri."]},{"k":"p","s":["Ini memperlihatkan kentalnya konsep ",["toxic masculinity","https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0191886925004210"]," (maskulinitas toksik), yakni norma maskulinitas yang menjadi merugikan ketika kekuatan, kontrol, dan ketangguhan diterjemahkan sebagai larangan untuk menunjukkan kerentanan."]},{"k":"p","s":[["Sejumlah penelitian","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27664823/"]," menunjukkan bahwa kepatuhan pada norma maskulinitas tradisional dapat membatasi ekspresi emosi dan membuat laki-laki lebih sulit mencari bantuan ketika menghadapi masalah emosional."]},{"k":"p","s":["Penelitian yang terbit pada 2026 menemukan bahwa toxic masculinity berkaitan kuat dengan ",["restrictive emotionality","https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0191886925004210"]," (pembatasan ekspresi emosi), yakni kecenderungan membatasi ekspresi emosi, yang pada gilirannya berkaitan dengan kecenderungan menghindari bantuan untuk persoalan emosional."]},{"k":"p","s":["Dengan kata lain, laki-laki mungkin bukannya “tidak mau cerita”. Mereka bisa saja ingin bercerita, tetapi ada harga sosial yang harus dibayar ketika mereka terlalu terbuka."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["Laki-laki juga rentan jadi korban kekerasan seksual, bukti kentalnya toxic masculinity dan budaya patriarki di Indonesia","https://theconversation.com/laki-laki-juga-rentan-jadi-korban-kekerasan-seksual-bukti-kentalnya-toxic-masculinity-dan-budaya-patriarki-di-indonesia-181968"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Jadi, laki-laki sebenarnya bercerita"]},{"k":"p","s":["Menariknya, kami juga menemukan bahwa para informan tetap memiliki ruang untuk berbagi kesedihan, terutama bersama teman laki-laki. Hanya, bentuknya jarang berupa percakapan terbuka mengenai perasaan, bukan pula berupa percakapan serius yang diawali dengan, “Gue lagi sedih banget.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["Mereka nongkrong hingga larut malam, bercanda, merokok, mendengarkan lagu-lagu sedih, bernyanyi bersama, atau sekadar duduk dan membicarakan hal-hal lain."]},{"k":"p","s":["Humor, misalnya, bukan semata-mata cara menghindari kesedihan. Lelucon memungkinkan pengalaman menyakitkan dibagikan tanpa seseorang harus secara eksplisit mengakui dirinya sedang rapuh."]},{"k":"p","s":["Musik juga menyediakan bahasa yang berbeda. Lagu patah hati memungkinkan seseorang mengekspresikan perasaan tanpa harus menjelaskan semuanya dengan kata-kata sendiri."]},{"k":"p","s":["Ini menunjukkan bahwa laki-laki sebenarnya tetap membutuhkan kedekatan emosional. Mereka hanya menegosiasikannya melalui bentuk-bentuk yang lebih mudah diterima dalam lingkungan maskulin."]},{"k":"p","s":["Temuan ini sejalan dengan kajian tentang ",["caring masculinities","https://doi.org/10.1177/1097184X15576203"]," (maskulinitas yang berorientasi pada kepedulian), yang menunjukkan bahwa relasi antarlaki-laki juga dapat menjadi ruang bagi kepedulian."]},{"k":"p","s":["Kepedulian tidak selalu hadir lewat percakapan emosional–bisa juga muncul melalui humor, kebersamaan, atau sekadar menemani teman."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["Bagaimana humor bisa mempengaruhi hubungan asmara Anda?","https://theconversation.com/bagaimana-humor-bisa-mempengaruhi-hubungan-asmara-anda-107158"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Maskulinitas butuh ‘kerja keras’"]},{"k":"p","s":["Patah hati memperlihatkan sebuah paradoks. Laki-laki sering diharapkan terlihat kuat dan stabil. Namun, untuk mempertahankan citra itu, mereka justru harus terus “kerja keras”: menahan tangis, memilih orang yang dipercaya, mengatur kapan kesedihan boleh muncul, dan mencari cara yang terasa aman untuk membagikannya."]},{"k":"p","s":["Anggapan bahwa “laki-laki tidak bercerita” bukan sekadar lelucon media sosial. Ia menjadi gambaran tentang bagaimana norma maskulinitas bekerja: bukan menghilangkan emosi laki-laki, tetapi menentukan emosi mana yang boleh terlihat dan bagaimana emosi itu boleh disampaikan."]},{"k":"p","s":["Sudah waktunya kita membongkar anggapan bahwa laki-laki harus menyembunyikan kesedihan agar tetap dianggap sebagai laki-laki."]},{"k":"p","s":["Menjadi laki-laki tidak mengharuskan seseorang menyamarkan kesedihannya. Ketangguhan dan kerentanan semestinya bisa terjadi bersamaan."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["Laki-laki yang menganut paham maskulinitas seksis lebih cenderung melecehkan perempuan","https://theconversation.com/laki-laki-yang-menganut-paham-maskulinitas-seksis-lebih-cenderung-melecehkan-perempuan-128312"]]}],"chars":6564},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/mauritius":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T17:03Z","sha":"bf18d84b3dc2930fcea482631a8800ae591e98ab26bff21db833a6a497ac9b60","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/mauritius","bytes":63698,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Mauritius travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-02-14T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-10-19T15:45:58+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/mauritius/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Mauritius’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Mauritius set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Mauritius High Commission in the UK","https://mauritius-london.govmu.org/Pages/index.aspx"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["Your passport must:"]},{"k":"li","s":["be valid for the duration of your stay"]},{"k":"li","s":["have one blank page"]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can visit Mauritius without a visa for up to 60 days. When you arrive, a border official will stamp your passport to show the end-date for your permitted stay."]},{"k":"p","s":["You must be able to show proof of onward or return travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["To stay longer (to work or study, for business travel or for other reasons), you must ",["meet the Mauritian government’s entry requirements","https://passport.govmu.org/passport/?page_id=605"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Digital travel form"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must fill in the ",["Mauritius All in One travel form","https://safemauritius.govmu.org/"]," online before you arrive. Print out a copy of your completed form with a QR code on it. You’ll need to show the form to officials when you arrive at the airport. If you do not fill in the form before you arrive, you’ll be asked to fill in an immigration disembarkation card at the airport."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["For details about health entry requirements and recommended vaccinations see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Mauritius guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/145/mauritius#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travelling from Reunion Island to Mauritius"]},{"k":"p","s":["On arrival in Mauritius, you may be asked questions about your health and recent travel history."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health screening"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you travel to Mauritius from a country where malaria is common, border officials may ask you for a blood sample to meet Ministry of Health requirements. If you are not tested at the airport, officials may contact you later during your visit."]},{"k":"h","s":["Ebola"]},{"k":"p","s":["As a precautionary measure, Mauritius has introduced entry restrictions in response to the Ebola outbreak:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Non-Mauritian nationals that have travelled from Democratic Republic of Congo or South Sudan within the preceding 21 days and that do not hold a valid work permit, residence permit, occupation permit, business, or student visa are not allowed to enter Mauritius"]},{"k":"li","s":["Mauritian nationals, and non-Mauritians holding a valid work permit, residence permit, occupation permit, business visa or student visa who have travelled from, transited through, or been present in Democratic Republic of Congo or South Sudan within the preceding 21 days will be permitted entry but will be subject to a 21-day mandatory quarantine"]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods you can take into or out of Mauritius","https://www.mra.mu/index.php/customs1/import/prohibited-restricted-goods"],". You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Mauritius"]},{"k":"p","s":["Although there’s no recent history of terrorism in Mauritius, attacks cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Most crime is non-violent such as burglaries from tourist villas or theft of unattended bags. Petty crime is common, particularly in tourist areas such as Port Louis, Grand Baie and Flic en Flac. There have been some reports of sexual assaults on tourists."]},{"k":"h","s":["Community unrest"]},{"k":"p","s":["Localised demonstrations and community unrest occurred in parts of Mauritius after a police shooting incident on 9 October 2026. This resulted in road closures, transport disruption and interruptions to public services."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Government of Mauritius introduced temporary curfews during the evenings and overnight, covering most areas of the main island, which lasted from 12 October to 18 October, ending at 4.30am on Monday 19 October."]},{"k":"p","s":["Stay away from demonstrations and large public gatherings and follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":[["You can contact our consular teams via telephone or email","https://www.gov.uk/government/collections/support-for-british-nationals-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting yourself and your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can take steps to reduce your personal risk by:"]},{"k":"li","s":["making sure your hotel room or accommodation is secure"]},{"k":"li","s":["keeping important documents such as your passport in a hotel safe"]},{"k":"li","s":["not walking alone at night on beaches or in poorly lit areas of town, particularly in the backstreets of the business district of Port Louis"]},{"k":"li","s":["only renting accommodation that is ",["registered with the Mauritius Tourism Authority","https://www.tourismauthority.mu/tourist-accommodation-certificate/"]]},{"k":"li","s":["not doing business with street or beach vendors"]},{"k":"p","s":["Report any incidents to the Mauritius tourist police by calling 999."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police may occasionally ask to see your ID, such as your passport or driving licence."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Cigarette paper"]},{"k":"p","s":["It’s illegal to possess or import cigarette papers."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs"]},{"k":"p","s":["Illegal drugs, including cannabis, carry severe penalties. Pack all luggage yourself and do not carry anything through customs for anyone else."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should expect a long jail sentence and heavy fines for possessing, using or smuggling illegal drugs, including when transiting through the airport."]},{"k":"p","s":["Airports in Mauritius have excellent technology and security for detecting illegal items. This is also used to scan the baggage of transiting passengers."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are found with any drugs, it’s unlikely you’ll be bailed, regardless of the type of drug. Prosecutions take a year or more to come to court, and you’ll be detained until your trial."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Police investigations"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re under police investigation, you’ll be provisionally charged and not allowed to leave the country without consent from a judge. It can take up to 2 years for the authorities to decide whether to issue a formal charge. You’re not allowed to renew your occupation or resident’s permit if you’re provisionally charged with a crime. If you cannot support yourself financially you’ll be kept in prison while the police finish their investigation."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex sexual activity was decriminalised in October 2023. Mauritius is a relatively conservative society and local attitudes to the LGBT+ community vary across the country. LGBT+ people still face discrimination, and verbal and physical confrontation cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Water sports"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re ",["taking part in any kind of water sports","https://www.rlss.org.uk/safety-on-holiday"],", make sure the:"]},{"k":"li","s":["operator holds a valid permit issued by the Ministry of Tourism"]},{"k":"li","s":["boat has enough life jackets on board"]},{"k":"li","s":["captain can contact the coastguard if needed"]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Mauritius, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["As in the UK, left-hand traffic is standard across Mauritius."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use a UK photocard driving licence to drive in Mauritius. Carry your licence with you when driving. If you still have a paper driving licence, you may need to ",["update it to a photocard licence","https://www.gov.uk/exchange-paper-driving-licence"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hire car companies often have stricter requirements for their customers, such as a year of driving experience, a higher minimum age and holding an IDP."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drivers are expected to carry in their vehicles yellow chalk, or other appropriate yellow indelible marker. This should be used to mark the position and registration number of their vehicle on the surface of a road in the event of an accident or breakdown, before moving their vehicle off the road where possible."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drink-driving is a serious offence in Mauritius. Penalties for driving under the influence of alcohol, even at very low levels (a ",["quarter of England’s legal limit of alcohol in your system","https://police.govmu.org/police/?page_id=6024"],"), can include fines or imprisonment."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Driving conditions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Accidents are common in Mauritius. Motorbikes are slow and often do not use headlights at night."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some roads are narrow and do not have pavements. Pedestrians and cyclists often use main roads, including motorways, during the day and at night."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Sea travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is still a significant risk of piracy in the Gulf of Aden and Indian Ocean, where piracy has been reduced but not prevented. Pirates can attack up to 1,000 nautical miles from the Somali coast or more."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling by sea, ",["read about the regional risks at sea","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/sea-river-and-piracy-safety"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to prepare for and respond to ",["extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Tropical cyclones"]},{"k":"p","s":["The tropical cyclone season in Mauritius usually runs from November to May."]},{"k":"p","s":["If a tropical cyclone happens, follow the advice of the ",["Mauritian government information service","https://gis.govmu.org/SitePages/Index.aspx"],". Mauritius Meteorological Services has a well-structured system of phased warnings."]},{"k":"p","s":["Cyclones can cause extensive damage to property. During a cyclone you’re not allowed to leave your accommodation, and car insurance policies often become invalid."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Landslides"]},{"k":"p","s":["Some areas are prone to landslides, especially during cyclones and torrential rains. Mauritius Meteorological Services has 5-stage landslide warnings. Local authorities may organise evacuations of threatened areas."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 999 or 114 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before you travel:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Mauritius","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/145/mauritius#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face on Mauritius","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/145/mauritius"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["dengue"]},{"k":"li","s":["Schistosomiasis"]},{"k":"li","s":["Chikungunya"]},{"k":"p","s":["Stonefish stings are rare but can be fatal. Get urgent medical attention if you are stung. Many hotels stock anti-venom serum."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can bring common medicines for your own use but you must carry a copy of the prescription. Tranquillisers, hypnotics, narcotics and other strong painkillers will require prior authorisation. You can check details with the ",["Mauritius Ministry of Health and Wellness","https://health.govmu.org/health/"],". If in any doubt, get advice from the ",["Mauritius High Commission in the UK","https://mauritius-london.govmu.org/Pages/index.aspx"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Mauritius"]},{"k":"p","s":["Good private healthcare is available but can be expensive. More complex cases may require evacuation to Réunion or South Africa. Make sure you have adequate travel health insurance and accessible funds to cover the cost of any medical treatment abroad and repatriation."]},{"k":"p","s":["Private and state ambulance services are available, but the quality and response speed can vary. If you can, go directly to the hospital."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in Mauritius","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/mauritius-list-of-doctors"]," where most staff will speak English."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Mauritius"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance: 999 or 114"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire: 999 or 115"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: 999 or 112"]},{"k":"h","s":["Tourist police"]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 999 or 112"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=mauritius"]," and ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=mauritius"]," in Mauritius"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Mauritius","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/mauritius-bereavement-information-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["Being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Mauritius","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/mauritius-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/mauritius/british-high-commission-port-louis"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/mauritius/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Mauritius","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-mauritius"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":14247,"change":"Updated information about local demonstrations and community unrest ('Safety and security' page)."},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/mauritania":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T17:03Z","sha":"fbef725130ca85ede150d603127bde7a134c798bc510f3e816827a463d4e4191","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/mauritania","bytes":52868,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Mauritania travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-01-30T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-10-19T20:07:15+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["Your travel insurance could be invalidated if you travel against Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) advice."]},{"k":"h","s":["Areas where FCDO advises against travel"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Eastern Mauritania"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to areas east of a line drawn between but excluding the towns of Kankossa, Akhreijit, Ghallaouia, Zouerat, Fderîck and onwards west to the border with Western Sahara."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Mauritania-Mali border"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to areas within 25km of the Malian border in the south, including Khabou."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Mauritania-Western Sahara border"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to areas within 25km of the Western Sahara border, excluding the section of the Nouakchott-Nouadhibou road and the city of Nouadhibou."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Tiris Zemmour, Adrar, Tagant, Hodh el Gharbi, Assaba, Gorgol and Guidimaka provinces"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the rest of the province of Tiris Zemmour, Assaba, Hodh el Gharbi, Gorgol and Guidimaka, and areas of Adrar and Tagant provinces, west of a line drawn between the towns of Ghallaouia, Akhreijit and Kankossa. This excludes:"]},{"k":"li","s":["the areas west of a line running between Choum, Ouadane and where the 10th Meridian West meets latitude 18° 40°"]},{"k":"li","s":["the town of Ouadane"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Tidjikja-Tichit road"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Aleg to Tidjikja road via Moudjeria and all areas of Tagant west of that road"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Bogue to Kaedi road via Bababe"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["why FCDO advises against travel","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/mauritania/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["UK government support"]},{"k":"p","s":["The British Embassy in Nouakchott cannot provide consular services in person. British nationals in Mauritania should contact the ",["British Embassy in Dakar, Senegal","https://www.gov.uk/world/organisations/british-embassy-dakar"],", for support."]},{"k":"h","s":["Protests"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been periodic protests in Mauritania relating to developments in the Middle East. Demonstrations can take place with little or no warning."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid all demonstrations and larger gatherings"]},{"k":"li","s":["monitor local media for updates"]},{"k":"li","s":["follow the advice of local authorities"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/mauritania/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK, who choose to travel despite FCDO advice. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Mauritania’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Mauritania set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Mauritanian Embassy in the UK","https://www.mauritania-embassy.uk/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Mauritania, your passport must have an ‘expiry date’ at least 6 months after the date you arrive."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["From 5 January 2025, it is no longer possible to obtain a visa on arrival in Mauritania. Before you travel, you must apply for an ",["e-visa","https://anrpts.gov.mr/visa/requestvisa"]," online via the website of the Mauritanian National Agency for the Population Register and Secure Titles (Agence Nationale du Registre des Populations et des Titres Sécurisés), who can be contacted via email ( contact@anrpts.gov.mr) if you face any technical difficulties with your application."]},{"k":"p","s":["E-visa fees must be paid on arrival in Mauritania in exact cash in Euros or US dollars. Change and receipts are not given. You should also print a copy of the e-visa and be ready to provide a copy on arrival in country. For information on visa lengths and fees, ",["read guidance on the Mauritanian government’s website","https://www.diplomatie.gov.mr/fr/node/50"]," (in French)."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should apply for an e-visa well in advance of your anticipated travel date to ensure that it is issued on time by the Mauritanian authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Mauritanian Embassy in London does not issue visas."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Mauritania, you must have a certificate to prove you’ve had a yellow fever vaccination if you’re coming from a ",["country listed as a transmission risk","https://nathnacyfzone.org.uk/factsheet/65/countries-with-risk-of-yellow-fever-transmission"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["For more details about health entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, including yellow fever, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Mauritania guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/144/mauritania#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about goods you can take into or out of Mauritania. You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Mauritania"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist attacks in Mauritania cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist attacks could be indiscriminate, including in places visited by foreign nationals. Stay aware of your surroundings, keep up to date with local media reports and follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["Demonstrations can happen at any time in Nouakchott. You should avoid political gatherings and demonstrations and follow the advice of local authorities and security forces. Maintain adequate stocks of non-perishable food and water."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Crime levels are moderate but steadily increasing."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been burglaries in areas where foreign nationals live and robberies during daylight."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been thefts and robberies at knifepoint during the day and night on the unlit and isolated beaches in Nouakchott and in the ‘Le Cinquième’, ‘Dar Naim’ and Citè Plage districts. Be particularly careful in these places."]},{"k":"p","s":["Report crime to the police by dialling 117 or visiting a police station."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["You should always carry copies of your photo ID, especially outside Nouakchott where there are police checkpoints. Follow any directions from the police or other Mauritanian security forces."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Religion"]},{"k":"p","s":["Mauritania recognises Islam as the only religion of its citizens and the state. It is illegal to participate in non-Islamic gatherings and activities that have not been authorised by the Mauritanian authorities. Attempting to convert people to other religions is illegal – those caught doing this can be arrested or deported. Blasphemy is a criminal offence and the maximum penalty is death. Respect local traditions, customs, laws and religion at all times and be aware of your actions to ensure they don’t offend, especially during the holy month of Ramadan or when visiting religious sites."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Ramadan"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ramadan is a holy month for Muslims. The dates vary by year and country. During this time, do not eat, drink, smoke or chew gum in public in the daytime, including in your car."]},{"k":"p","s":["Get more advice when you arrive from your tour guide, hotel or business contacts."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check opening hours of shops and restaurants"]},{"k":"li","s":["follow local dress codes – clothing that does not meet local dress codes may cause more offence at this time"]},{"k":"li","s":["be aware that driving may be more erratic, particularly when people are trying to get home at dusk"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Alcohol and pork products ban"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal for Muslims and non-Muslims to import or consume alcohol and pork products in Mauritania. The Mauritanian authorities scan all baggage on arrival at Nouakchott International Airport and may search vehicles at land borders. They will confiscate alcohol or pork products and arrest or fine people found carrying them."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs and prison sentences"]},{"k":"p","s":["Drug laws in Mauritania are severe. The penalties for possessing illegal drugs include imprisonment."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Dress code"]},{"k":"p","s":["You should dress modestly when not on the beach. Women should cover their shoulders and not wear short skirts. Men should not wear sleeveless tops or shorts."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Using cameras"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police and members of the public can object to photography or filming if you do not get their permission. Do not photograph or film government and military sites or members of the security forces."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are no laws in Mauritania to protect LGBT+ people from discrimination."]},{"k":"p","s":["Under Mauritanian law, the maximum penalty for sexual activity between men is death. Penalties for women include a prison sentence ranging from 3 months to 2 years and a fine."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Accessing money"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to take Mauritanian currency (ougiyas) out of the country. The Mauritanian authorities may confiscate money from people trying to do so."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can exchange US dollars and euros for Mauritanian ouguiyas at banks, some hotels and official bureau de change. You can exchange British pounds at some official bureau de change, but the exchange rate is very poor."]},{"k":"p","s":["Mauritania is mainly a cash-based society. A few hotels in Nouakchott and Nouadhibou accept credit cards – some hotels only accept Visa cards. Some ATMs accept foreign credit or debit cards."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Mauritania, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use a UK photocard driving licence to drive in Mauritania. If you still have a paper driving licence, you may need to ",["update it to a photocard licence","https://www.gov.uk/exchange-paper-driving-licence"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Driving standards"]},{"k":"p","s":["Driving standards can be poor in Mauritania, traffic rules are rarely respected and paved roads are generally in poor condition."]},{"k":"p","s":["Overland travel is difficult, and you should use a 4-wheel-drive vehicle and avoid travelling at night. Check the tide times on coastal roads and travel in convoy if possible. Carry out a maintenance check before you travel and make sure you have enough water and fuel on long journeys."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should travel in a convoy with an experienced guide if leaving urban areas, including to the tourist sites of Chinguetti and Tichit. You should carry sufficient supplies and inform family or friends of your travel plans."]},{"k":"p","s":["Public transport vehicles are poorly maintained, especially in the interior of the country."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Iron Ore Train"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to travel on the iron ore train outside the designated passenger carriages. Doing so carries a significant risk of serious injury, and there are no adequate medical facilities in the region to provide emergency treatment."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Crossing borders"]},{"k":"p","s":["Crossing the border into Mauritania can be time-consuming, and officials may ask for payments before they allow you to cross. Check local advice before travelling to neighbouring countries in case border crossings are closed or congested."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional Risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["This section has safety advice for regions of Mauritania. It only covers regions where FCDO has specific advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/mauritania"]," and ",["safety and security advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/mauritania/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Eastern Mauritania"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to areas east of a line drawn between but excluding the towns of Kankossa, Akhreijit, Ghallaouia, Zouerat, Fderîck and onwards west to the border with Western Sahara."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel because of designated “military zones” by the Mauritanian military. These areas are off-limits to foreign nationals and most civilians. The Mauritanian Ministry of Defence warned that individuals entering these areas would be treated as a military target. The boundaries of such zones frequently change and authorisation from authorities is needed for access."]},{"k":"p","s":["These zones are due to the deterioration of the security situation in Mali leading to increased risks of incursions by terrorist groups based in Mali into Mauritanian territory, including Jama’a Nusrat ul-Islam wa al-Muslimin (JNIM). Serious organised crime groups operate in eastern Mauritania."]},{"k":"h","s":["Mauritania-Mali border"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to areas within 25km of the Malian border in the south of Mauritania, including Khabou. There is increased terrorist and serious organised crime activity across the border in Kayes Region in Mali."]},{"k":"h","s":["Mauritania-Western Sahara border"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to areas within 25km of the Western Sahara border, excluding the section of the Nouakchott-Nouadhibou road and the city of Nouadhibou."]},{"k":"p","s":["Unexploded landmines remain on the border with Western Sahara. Landmines can shift location due to weather conditions. You should check local advice before travelling."]},{"k":"h","s":["Tiris Zemmour, Adrar, Tagant, Hodh el Gharbi, Assaba and Guidimaka provinces"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the rest of the province of Tiris Zemmour, Assaba, Hodh el Gharbi, Gorgol and Guidimaka, and areas of Adrar and Tagant provinces, west of a line drawn between the towns of Ghallaouia, Akhreijit and Kankossa. This excludes:"]},{"k":"li","s":["the areas west of a line running between Choum, Ouadane and where the 10th Meridian West meets latitude 18° 40°"]},{"k":"li","s":["the town of Ouadane"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Tidjikja-Tichit road"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Aleg to Tidjikja road via Moudjeria and all areas of Tagant west of that road"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Bogue to Kaedi road via Bababe"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is due to risks of instability extending from eastern Mauritania as a result of the conflict in Mali, including from organised crime."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 101 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Mauritania authorities may not answer calls to this number."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Mauritania","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/144/mauritania#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Mauritania","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/144/mauritania"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["dengue"]},{"k":"li","s":["yellow fever"]},{"k":"li","s":["biting insects"]},{"k":"li","s":["Rift Valley fever"]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["Many medicines may be fake or hard to find in Mauritania. Carry enough of your prescription medication for your stay. The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Mauritania"]},{"k":"p","s":["Medical facilities are extremely limited in Mauritania, particularly outside Nouakchott and Nouadhibou, where lack of communications makes dealing with an emergency very difficult."]},{"k":"p","s":["Clinics in Nouakchott and Nouadhibou charge for medical care (sometimes in euros or US dollars) and may not accept foreign insurance cards. Make sure you have adequate travel health insurance and accessible funds to cover the cost of any medical treatment abroad and repatriation."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in Mauritania","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/mauritania-doctors"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Mauritania"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance: 101"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire: 118"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: 117"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Mauritania authorities may not answer calls to these numbers."]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=mauritania"]," in Mauritania"]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Mauritania","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/mauritania-prisoner-pack--2"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/mauritania/british-embassy-nouakchott"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/mauritania/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]}],"chars":17270,"change":"New information on risks of travelling on the iron ore train (‘Safety and security’ page)."},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/san-marino":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T17:18Z","sha":"0ad89cac73454df15a0fb33e8cde9f1938d8559ec6e8221d2b9a618bf596bc60","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/san-marino","bytes":38213,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"San Marino travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-03-21T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2025-12-10T13:33:32+00:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"p","s":["You will need to travel through Italy to enter San Marino. Read ",["Italy travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/italy"]," before you visit San Marino."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/san-marino/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of the current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in San Marino set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["San Marino Consulate in the UK","https://www.consolatosanmarino.uk/en/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travelling through Italy"]},{"k":"p","s":["You will need to travel through Italy to enter San Marino. Read ",["Italy travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/italy"]," before you visit."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["San Marino follows ",["Schengen area rules","https://europa.eu/youreurope/citizens/travel/entry-exit/non-eu-nationals/index_en.htm"],". Your passport must:"]},{"k":"li","s":["have a ‘date of issue’ less than 10 years before the date you arrive – if you renewed your passport before 1 October 2018, it may have a date of issue that is more than 10 years ago"]},{"k":"li","s":["have an ‘expiry date’ at least 3 months after the day you plan to leave the Schengen area"]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"p","s":["You do not need to pass through border controls to enter San Marino, but you must have a passport containing a valid entry stamp from a Schengen area country."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can travel without a visa to the Schengen area and San Marino for up to 90 days in any 180-day period. This applies if you travel:"]},{"k":"li","s":["as a tourist"]},{"k":"li","s":["to visit family or friends"]},{"k":"li","s":["to attend business meetings, cultural or sports events"]},{"k":"li","s":["for short-term studies or training"]},{"k":"p","s":["The requirements for working in San Marino are different. Contact the ",["San Marino Consulate in the UK","https://www.consolatosanmarino.uk/en/uk-citizens/"]," for more information."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling to other Schengen area countries as well, make sure your whole visit is within the 90-day limit. Visits to Schengen countries in the 180 days before you travel count towards your 90 days."]},{"k":"h","s":["Registering with the police"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must go to the Gendarmerie to register and get a permit of stay if you are visiting for more than 30 days or are planning to work. Check the ",["San Marino Ministry of Foreign Affairs","https://www.esteri.sm/pub1/EsteriSM/en/Servizi-per-utenza/Informazioni-per-chi-visita-SMR.html"]," website for more information."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["For details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s San Marino guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/189/san-marino#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["The rules about goods that can be brought into and taken out of San Marino are the same as for Italy. You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in San Marino"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist attacks in San Marino cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Street crime is very rare, but you should take precautions to keep your passport and money safe."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Alcohol and drink spiking"]},{"k":"p","s":["Drinks served in bars abroad are often stronger than those in the UK. Be careful about accepting drinks from strangers or leaving your drinks unattended."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs and prison sentences"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a zero-tolerance policy towards the possession and use of illegal drugs. If you are convicted, you will face a prison sentence or a heavy fine."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in San Marino, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"]," and check the ",["rules of the road in the RAC’s San Marino guide","https://www.rac.co.uk/drive/travel/country/san-marino/"],". The guide lists driving regulations and other legal requirements you need to be aware of."]},{"k":"p","s":["You’ll need to have both the ",["1968 version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," and your UK driving licence with you in the car. You cannot buy an IDP outside the UK, so get one before you travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hire car companies often have stricter requirements for their customers, such as a year of driving experience and a higher minimum age."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 118 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for San Marino","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/189/san-marino#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in San Marino","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/189/san-marino"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in San Marino"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you need urgent medical attention, you can visit the ",["San Marino state hospital","https://www.iss.sm/on-line/home/dipartimento-ospedaliero.html"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Health insurance cards"]},{"k":"p","s":["The European Health Insurance Card (EHIC) and Global Health Insurance Card (GHIC) are not accepted in San Marino. Take out comprehensive travel and medical insurance before travelling."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in San Marino"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance: 118"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire: 115"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: 112 or 113"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Italy"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Italy"],", ",["medical providers","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/italy-list-of-medical-facilitiespractitioners/list-of-english-speaking-doctors-in-italy"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=Italy"]," in Italy"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Italy","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/bereavement-information-for-italy"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested in Italy","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/italy-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/san-marino/british-embassy-rome"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/san-marino/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]}],"chars":8334,"change":"New information about dual nationals returning to the UK (‘Entry requirements’ page)."},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/venezuela":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-20T17:36Z","sha":"d2ac66f742ef14f353efbe91c21aae0129c6f14b2e3498c49f5824a6ac318ca7","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/venezuela","bytes":76478,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Venezuela travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-03-13T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-11-18T11:13:15+00:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["Your travel insurance could be invalidated if you travel against advice from the Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO)."]},{"k":"h","s":["Areas where FCDO advises against all travel"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Border areas"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["within 40km (25 miles) of the border with Colombia"]},{"k":"li","s":["within 40km (25 miles) of the border with Brazil"]},{"k":"li","s":["within 40km (25 miles) of the border with Guyana"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Amazonas"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to Amazonas state."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Bolívar"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to the Orinoco Mining Arc (‘Arco Minero’) region to the south of the Orinoco river. This includes:"]},{"k":"li","s":["all of Cedeño Municipality"]},{"k":"li","s":["the northern part of Sucre Municipality, from the Orinoco river to 80km south of the Troncal 19 road"]},{"k":"li","s":["the northern part of Raul Leoni Municipality, north of the Rio Paragua"]},{"k":"li","s":["all of Piar Municipality"]},{"k":"li","s":["all of Roscio Municipality"]},{"k":"li","s":["all of El Callo Municipality"]},{"k":"li","s":["all of Sifontes Municipality"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Delta Amacuro"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to the area to the south of the Orinoco river."]},{"k":"p","s":["The FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the remaining areas of Venezuela including Zulia state, Maracaibo and the eastern shore of Lake Maracaibo."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are a British national already living or travelling in Venezuela, you should be prepared to change your plans quickly if necessary. You should have a ‘personal emergency plan’, including practical steps for leaving the country or staying safe during a crisis, that does not depend on support from the UK government."]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel, ",["check the ‘Entry requirements’ section","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/venezuela/entry-requirements#entry-rules-in-response-to-coronavirus-covid-19"]," for Venezuela’s current entry restrictions and requirements. These may change with little warning. Monitor local media and stay in contact with your travel provider."]},{"k":"h","s":["Earthquakes near coast of Venezuela"]},{"k":"p","s":["On 24 June, Venezuela suffered two earthquakes of 7.2 and 7.5 magnitude, causing significant damage in La Guaira, parts of Caracas and elsewhere."]},{"k":"p","s":["A significant humanitarian response is currently taking place. If in Venezuela, follow the advice of local authorities and stay away from damaged buildings."]},{"k":"h","s":["Air Travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["Availability of commercial flights to and from Venezuela is limited. Maiquetía Simón Bolívar International Airport, which serves Caracas, resumed commercial flight operations on 1 September with limited capacity."]},{"k":"p","s":["Commercial international flights are also operating from Valencia (Arturo Michelena International Airport), Barquisimeto (Jacinto Lara International Airport), Barcelona (General José Antonio Anzoátegui International Airport) and Maracaibo (La Chinita International Airport). Stay in contact with your airline or travel agent/tour operator for the latest travel information and flight updates."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are affected and need assistance from the UK government, call:"]},{"k":"li","s":["from Venezuela +58 (0) 212 263 8411"]},{"k":"li","s":["from UK +44 (0) 207 008 5000"]},{"k":"p","s":["Be aware of the possibility of earthquake aftershocks. See further information about ",["earthquakes","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/venezuela/safety-and-security#earthquakes"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and get ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out more about ",["FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/venezuela/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Venezuela’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Venezuela set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the Venezuelan Embassy in the UK (in Spanish)."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Venezuela, your passport must have an ‘expiry date’ at least 6 months after the date you plan to leave."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/apply-renew-passport"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re a resident in Venezuela, both your passport and resident visa or permit must be valid at the time of your arrival."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you arrive by air, you can stay for up to 90 days visa-free on a ‘tourist card’, issued on arrival. Upon arrival, you must show proof of:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your accommodation, either a private accommodation booking or a notarised invitation letter issued via ",["SAREN","https://www.saren.gob.ve/"]," (Servicio Autónomo de Registros y Notarías) from relatives or friends you may be staying with"]},{"k":"li","s":["available funds to cover the length of your visit"]},{"k":"li","s":["a return or onward travel ticket"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re arriving overland or by sea, you may need to get a visa in advance. Contact the nearest ",["Venezuelan embassy or consulate","https://mppre.gob.ve/en/venezuelan-embassies-in-the-world/"]," for advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can apply for an extension of up to 90 days before your tourist card and entry stamp expire. Apply and pay the fee at any SAIME (immigration service) office (in Spanish). If you live in Venezuela, you must apply at the main regional SAIME office of the state where you are living."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you overstay your allowed time, you may be arrested and fined when trying to leave Venezuela."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you aren’t visiting for tourism, you must get a visa before travelling to Venezuela. It must be related to the purpose and duration of your stay. This includes visits related to work of any nature, whether paid or unpaid, including for:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Non-governmental organisations (NGOs)"]},{"k":"li","s":["journalism"]},{"k":"li","s":["documentary or filmmaking"]},{"k":"li","s":["volunteering"]},{"k":"p","s":["You will also need a visa if you are coming to Venezuela to study or for an internship."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact the nearest ",["Venezuelan embassy or consulate","https://mppre.gob.ve/embajadas"]," for information on how to obtain a visa."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationality"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re a Venezuelan dual national, you must use your Venezuelan identity documents for entry and departure of the country. The authorities may not allow you to depart Venezuela if you are not in possession of a valid Venezuelan passport, as required by law."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travelling with children"]},{"k":"p","s":["Children travelling unaccompanied, with a guardian, or with one parent, must provide a travel permit from the non-travelling parent(s). This permit is available from the nearest local ",["SAREN (Servicio Autónomo de Registros y Notarías)","https://www.saren.gob.ve/"]," office, or the respective Court for Children and Adolescents."]},{"k":"p","s":["If the child is resident overseas, you can get the permit from the nearest ",["Venezuelan embassy or consulate","https://mppre.gob.ve/embajadas/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The child should carry this travel permit while travelling within the country and also at time of departure from Venezuela. Children may be denied exit from the country otherwise."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Venezuela from Brazil, you must have a certificate to prove you’ve had a yellow fever vaccination."]},{"k":"p","s":["For full details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Venezuela guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/239/venezuela#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about goods you can take into or out of Venezuela. You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to import, export, sell, possess and use any vaping devices, including those that do not contain nicotine. You may get a large fine."]},{"k":"p","s":["You must declare any goods, including personal luggage, worth more than 1,000 US dollars. You may have to pay import tax if your goods appear to be new."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking money into Venezuela"]},{"k":"p","s":["Declare cash or travellers cheques worth 10,000 US dollars or more when entering Venezuela. You must pay tax on the excess."]},{"k":"p","s":["Most local businesses accept payment in local currency, US dollars or by international card. Some card machines do not work with international cards."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you find your bank cards do not work, you can arrange for friends and family overseas to deposit funds through Western Union and pick up the deposited funds at any ",["Grupo Zoom","https://zoom.red/remesas/"]," branch."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"p","s":["This guide also has safety advice for ",["regions of Venezuela","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/venezuela/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Venezuela"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist attacks in Venezuela cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Political and Economic situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["On 15 September, the UK upgraded diplomatic relations with Venezuela. Although the security situation has improved in recent years and many visits take place without incident, political, economic and operational conditions remain uncertain and can change rapidly. Consular access and support to British nationals in Venezuela may be severely limited. If you choose to travel to Venezuela, monitor developments closely through local and international media and keep up to date with this travel advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["Flights can be delayed or cancelled at short notice. Keep in close contact with your airline or travel agent to see if your flight is affected. Some foreign nationals have reported extensive questioning by the authorities on arrival or departure. This can result in delays and, in some cases, may affect onward travel arrangements."]},{"k":"p","s":["Following the earthquakes of 24 June, La Guaira was declared a disaster zone and remains subject to enhanced security and access controls. There is also a visible security presence in major cities, including Caracas, and security checkpoints are common throughout the country. Foreign nationals may be asked to provide identification or explain the purpose of their visit. British nationals should always carry identification and ensure that travel documentation is in order. Members of the security services are unlikely to speak English."]},{"k":"p","s":["Public services including healthcare and security, and the supply of electricity, water and fuel can be severely limited, particularly outside of Caracas. Prolonged power cuts can affect living conditions and cut mobile networks and internet."]},{"k":"h","s":["Protests and demonstrations"]},{"k":"p","s":["Demonstrations and gatherings can turn violent and could be dispersed by the use of force, including with the use of rubber bullets and tear gas. Avoid political rallies, demonstrations and crowds, and do not cross security-force lines or barricades."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Armed robbery, mugging, carjacking and burglary are common in parts of Venezuela and can be accompanied by extreme violence. Do not resist an attacker. Remain alert and avoid displaying electronics or valuables while on the street or in a vehicle. Avoid public transport. A number of robberies, including at gunpoint, have taken place on public transport."]},{"k":"p","s":["Use pre-booked taxis only. Hotels will normally book a taxi from a reputable company or supply their own service."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Crime in rural areas"]},{"k":"p","s":["In many rural areas, the authority of the Venezuelan state is limited, and the British Embassy’s ability to help British nationals in trouble in these areas will also be limited. Mobile and internet connection in rural areas is often limited."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Armed groups"]},{"k":"p","s":["Illegal armed groups and criminal gangs are active in states along Venezuela’s borders and where illegal mining or drugs trafficking is common. The risk is particularly significant in:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Zulia, Tachira and Apure states"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Orinoco Mining Arc area of Amazonas, Bolívar and Delta Amacuro states, including the Orinoco River"]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["Always carry identification with you. You can carry copies of the photo page of your passport and your entry stamp. Keep your passport in a safe place where you can access it quickly if you need to. Local security forces may ask you to show ID at any time, particularly at police checkpoints. If you cannot produce it, you could be arrested and detained pending an investigation."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs and prison sentences"]},{"k":"p","s":["Drug trafficking is a serious crime in Venezuela. Detection methods are sophisticated, and drug traffickers should expect to be arrested. Conviction leads to a lengthy prison sentence of between 8 and 15 years. Conditions in Venezuelan jails are harsh, dangerous, and among the worst in the region."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Using cameras and binoculars in secure areas"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to photograph military or strategic installations, including military airports and the presidential palace. Avoid plane spotting."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Networks and internet"]},{"k":"p","s":["Internet service is generally slow outside of major cities. Mobile network operators run modern 4G (LTE) networks in most major cities, while they also operate slower legacy 3G (WCDMA) networks for their nationwide voice and data services."]},{"k":"p","s":["In Venezuela, power outages are frequent and can occur without warning, often due to infrastructure challenges and weather conditions. These can be both localised and nationwide, and may vary in duration. Power cuts can affect mobile signals and internet across the whole country, including Caracas. Keep in contact with your airline or travel agent as flights can be delayed or cancelled when there are power cuts."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex relationships are not illegal. However, showing affection in public will most likely be frowned on by locals and may receive negative attention from the police and other security forces. Venezuelan law does not recognise same-sex partnerships, so same-sex partners have no rights as next of kin."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Age of consent"]},{"k":"p","s":["The age of consent in Venezuela is 18 and sex with anyone 17 and under is illegal. Offenders face long jail sentences."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Swimming safety"]},{"k":"p","s":["The waters of the Caribbean can be deceptive. There are strong currents and undertows in some areas that can make swimming hazardous. Lifeguards and warnings are not normally in place."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["water safety on holiday","https://www.rlss.org.uk/safety-on-holiday"]," from the Royal Life Saving Society."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travelling to and from Maiquetia Airport, Caracas"]},{"k":"p","s":["Be especially vigilant around Maiquetia airport due to the risk of criminal activity. Some travellers have previously been targeted at the arrival or departure terminals, at the airport’s car park and during travel to or from the airport. If you show signs of wealth this could lead to targeting by criminals."]},{"k":"p","s":["You are at most risk when travelling to and from the airport at night. There have previously been armed robberies on buses on this route. Ideally, arrange for friends, business contacts or your tour operator to meet you at the airport. If that’s not possible, consider travelling by licensed taxi from the official taxi rank outside the arrivals hall. Do not accept offers of transport in the arrivals hall and do not get into a taxi if there are passengers already in the car."]},{"k":"p","s":["The National Guard carries out random drug and security checks at Maiquetia Airport, particularly on departure. Departing passengers are sometimes asked to accompany an officer to a local hospital for an X-ray. Beware of fake security officials. If you’re in any doubt ask other airline or airport staff."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive a hire car or a UK vehicle, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use a UK photocard driving licence to drive in Venezuela for up to one year, and after that you will need a Venezuelan driving licence. If you still have a paper driving licence, you may need to ",["update it to a photocard licence","https://www.gov.uk/exchange-paper-driving-licence"]," or get the ",["correct version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," as well."]},{"k":"p","s":["Heavy rains and lack of maintenance can affect road conditions. Get local advice about your route before you set out, leave plenty of time for your journey and keep to the main roads. Avoid travelling after dark. All vehicles must carry a spare tyre, wheel block, jack and reflector triangle."]},{"k":"p","s":["Fuel shortages are common across all parts of the country outside Caracas. Not all petrol stations accept international cards so you may need to pay with local currency or US dollars. Pay the correct price, as petrol stations do not usually give any change. You should check the local situation ahead of any travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["There are regular police and National Guard checkpoints throughout the country. Drive slowly through these and stop if asked. There have been reports of attempts by the police and National Guard to extract bribes at check points, including for traffic offences."]},{"k":"p","s":["Driving under the influence of alcohol is illegal but common, especially at weekends. Many vehicles are in poor condition and drivers routinely ignore red lights."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re in a traffic accident, you must stay at the scene until the traffic police arrive. Police can seize your vehicle if you cannot show them your insurance documents, driving licence and passport. You can show a copy of your passport as long as you can show both the photo page and the entry stamp. Insurance companies will not pay claims on vehicles that have been moved without a police accident report."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Air travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["Availability of flights to and from Venezuela remains limited. Stay in contact with your airline or travel agent/tour operator for up-to-date information on your itinerary."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["UK Air Safety List (ASL)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/uk-air-safety-list"]," lists all known airlines in Venezuela that do not meet international safety standards and are banned from operating commercial air services to and from the UK. Check the ASL when considering which airlines to fly with. The list is maintained by the Department for Transport, based on advice from the ",["UK Civil Aviation Authority","https://www.caa.co.uk/Commercial-Industry/Airlines/Licensing/Requirements-and-guidance/Third-Country-Operator-Certificates"],". The ",["UK Civil Aviation Authority","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/uk-air-safety-list"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check whether your tour operator has concerns about airlines in Venezuela."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some flights have previously been delayed or cancelled at short notice. Keep in close contact with your airline or travel agent to see if your flight is affected."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Sea travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been incidents of piracy and armed robbery in and around Venezuela’s waters, especially east of Puerto La Cruz and in waters between Venezuela and Trinidad and Tobago."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling by sea, ",["read about the regional risks at sea","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/sea-river-and-piracy-safety"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to ",["prepare for and respond to extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Hurricanes"]},{"k":"p","s":["The hurricane season runs from June to November and affects parts of northern Venezuela. Follow the weather reports on local TV and radio. You can also monitor weather updates online from the World Meteorological Organization and the US National Hurricane Center. Always follow the advice of the local authorities."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Flooding"]},{"k":"p","s":["During the rainy season from May to November there may be flooding in low-lying areas (for example, the Llanos) and in some valleys in the Andes, mainly in Merida State. There may be disruption to transport, services and infrastructure."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Earthquakes"]},{"k":"p","s":["On 24 June, Venezuela suffered two earthquakes of 7.2 and 7.5 magnitude, causing significant damage in La Guaira, parts of Caracas and elsewhere."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is an ongoing risk of earthquakes in Venezuela. Monitor media reports and follow the advice of the local authorities. The US Federal Emergency Management Agency website has advice about ",["what to do before, during and after an earthquake","https://www.ready.gov/earthquakes"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["This section has safety advice for regions of Venezuela. It only covers regions where Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) has specific advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/venezuela"]," and ",["safety and security advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/venezuela/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Venezuela’s borders"]},{"k":"p","s":["Drug traffickers and illegal armed groups are active along the border areas with Colombia, Brazil and Guyana."]},{"k":"p","s":["Avoid crossing between Venezuela and Colombia by land."]},{"k":"p","s":["Crossing the border from Brazil can be a lengthy process."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is no official land border crossing between Venezuela and Guyana, which is covered by dense jungle. Avoid crossing between Venezuela and Guyana by land."]},{"k":"h","s":["Bolívar"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to the Orinoco Mining Arc (‘Arco Minero’) region to the south of the Orinoco river. This includes:"]},{"k":"li","s":["all of Cedeño Municipality"]},{"k":"li","s":["the northern part of Sucre Municipality, from the Orinoco river to 80km south of the Troncal 19 road"]},{"k":"li","s":["all of Heres Municpality"]},{"k":"li","s":["the northern part of Raul Leoni Municipality, north of the Rio Paragua"]},{"k":"li","s":["all of Piar Municipality"]},{"k":"li","s":["all of Roscio Municipality"]},{"k":"li","s":["all of El Callo Municipality"]},{"k":"li","s":["all of Sifontes Municipality"]},{"k":"p","s":["Illegal armed groups are active in the ‘Arco Minero’ and involved in criminal activities, particularly in remote areas."]},{"k":"h","s":["Delta Amacuro"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to the area to the south of the Orinoco river, where drug traffickers and illegal armed groups are active."]},{"k":"h","s":["Zulia"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to Zulia state due to the presence of illegal armed groups and ongoing crime and violence."]},{"k":"hs","s":["All other areas of Venezuela"]},{"k":"p","s":["Almost all states outside Caracas are experiencing issues, including criminality, prolonged power cuts, water and fuel shortages and a general lack of essential services."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a pre-existing health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 911 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Nationwide ambulance services like AeroAm (in Spanish) can help you get back to Caracas or other major cities if you’re in an accident."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Venezuela","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/239/venezuela#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Venezuela","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/239/venezuela"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["yellow fever"]},{"k":"li","s":["dengue"]},{"k":"li","s":["schistosomiasis (a parasitic infection)."]},{"k":"p","s":["Altitude sickness is a risk in parts of Venezuela, mainly in the Andean region of the country: Mérida, Táchira and Trujillo states. Read more about ",["altitude sickness on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/26/altitude-illness"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Venezuelan Pharmaceutical Federation has reported a shortage of 80% of medical supplies due to the economic crisis. Make sure you bring enough medication for your stay, including extra medication in case your return is delayed."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare facilities in Venezuela"]},{"k":"p","s":["Medical facilities in Venezuela vary widely in quality. In large cities, private clinics provide acceptable care for routine treatments but are vulnerable to acute shortages of medical supplies and medicines. More complex treatments may require evacuation. Make sure you have adequate travel health insurance and accessible funds to cover the cost of any medical treatment abroad and repatriation."]},{"k":"p","s":["Public health facilities are poor, with frequent shortages of medicines and funding. Ambulance services in remote areas are unreliable and hospitals often lack adequate medical supplies and equipment."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a list of ",["English-speaking doctors in Venezuela","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/venezuela-list-of-medical-facilities-practitioners"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Venezuela"]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 911 (ambulance, fire, police)"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Venezuela"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Venezuela"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=Venezuela"]]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Venezuela","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/bereavement-information-for-venezuela/venezuela-bereavement-information--2"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Venezuela","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/venezuela-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of rape or sexual assault","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/venezuela-information-for-victims-of-rape-and-sexual-assault"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/venezuela/british-embassy-caracas"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/venezuela/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Venezuela","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-venezuela"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":25364,"change":"Updated information about documents you must show to immigration authorities upon arrival and the ban on any vaping device, including those that do not contain nicotine (‘Entry requirements’ page)."},"https://theconversation.com/the-good-the-bad-and-the-biased-the-medias-mixed-report-card-on-the-victorian-election-297285":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-26T05:34Z","sha":"23d3cad163cf502859eec62f47eafa00125c03d5b3b376a8cf3dc3a0fb8c2407","read":"https://theconversation.com/the-good-the-bad-and-the-biased-the-medias-mixed-report-card-on-the-victorian-election-297285","bytes":183643,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"The good, the bad and the biased: the media’s mixed report card on the Victorian election","by":[{"n":"Denis Muller","r":"Senior Research Fellow, Centre for Advancing Journalism and Information Integrity, The University of Melbourne"}],"pub":"2026-11-26T04:52:00Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Nothing the media did was going to make any difference to the outcome of this Victorian state election, but what the coverage did show was the stark contrast between Australia’s two main commercial media outfits in their desire and capacity to push an agenda."]},{"k":"p","s":["News Corporation, through the Herald Sun, The Australian and News24, ran its usual conservative, anti-Labor agenda with disciplined, technical professionalism allied to a complete disregard for the ethic of impartiality."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Nine Entertainment Company, through The Age, was far more diffuse."]},{"k":"p","s":["Without running an anti-Labor agenda, it had done immense damage to the Labor cause with its excellent reporting over two years into the monstrous corruption engulfing the Big Build. Throughout the campaign, it gave constant front-page coverage to the ensuing royal commission."]},{"k":"p","s":["Two days out from the election, its page 1 headline was: “Inquiry told of CFMEU debauchery”, a form of words abundantly supported by the ",["allegations of bribery","https://www.theage.com.au/national/victoria/ping-pong-sex-parties-and-payment-in-cocaine-secret-witness-s-explosive-testimony-reveals-cfmeu-demands-20261125-p61h2y.html"]," and thuggery in the story beneath."]},{"k":"p","s":["This was just good public interest journalism."]},{"k":"p","s":["It also published an ",["entertaining account","https://www.theage.com.au/politics/victoria/what-is-a-man-leaked-talking-points-lay-bare-tightly-controlled-coalition-campaign-20261122-p61g27.html"]," of the briefing notes assigned to Liberal candidates to try to minimise the risk they would be tripped up on issues such as transgender rights and the price of milk, illustrating the tight control of a party determined to protect itself and its leader from troubling gaffes."]},{"k":"p","s":["Here, it was also making a broader point about the way politicians are robotically controlled at the expense of candour when dealing with the media."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, when it came to One Nation, The Age was absolutely all over the place."]},{"k":"p","s":["In fact, this was the single most bizarre feature of the entire election coverage."]},{"k":"p","s":["One minute, The Age was giving One Nation huge propaganda boosts on its front page, and the next minute, it was headlining yet more One Nation dysfunction, financial problems and ideological ugliness."]},{"k":"p","s":["First, the propaganda."]},{"k":"p","s":["Page 1 of The Sunday Age of ",["November 15","https://www.theage.com.au/politics/victoria/meet-the-sri-lankan-immigration-lawyer-handing-out-for-pauline-hanson-at-the-election-20261114-p61d90.html"]," was dominated by a photo of Lalin de Silva, originally from Sri Lanka, who we were told intends to vote for One Nation. He was photographed against a backdrop of the Australian flag."]},{"k":"p","s":["The subtext was that if an immigrant from South Asia was prepared to vote for One Nation, perhaps it is not as racist as we all thought."]},{"k":"p","s":["This is not to criticise de Silva’s voting choice, but The Age story flies in the face of recent ",["research by the Roy Morgan","https://www.roymorgan.com/findings/10341-voting-intentions-by-country-of-birth-september-2026"]," organisation showing immigrants from Asia are the least likely of Australia’s ethnic communities to vote for One Nation."]},{"k":"p","s":["The survey research showed that electors born in mainland China, Nepal, Pakistan, Vietnam, Sri Lanka and India are all far less likely for vote for One Nation than Australian voters as a whole. Including these data would at least have given the story some context."]},{"k":"p","s":["And then on November 20, The Age’s page 1 carried a large photo of One Nation’s Victoria leader looking very sure of himself under a headline “The smiling assassin”. This was reinforced by a double-page spread on page 4 headlined, “How Pickering wants to soften One Nation and crush its rivals”."]},{"k":"p","s":["The idea was to show that as One Nation’s Victorian leader, Warren Pickering was responsive to what was assumed to be a greater sensitivity among Victoria voters about his party’s divisiveness than it might have become accustomed to elsewhere."]},{"k":"p","s":["And now the real news."]},{"k":"p","s":["On November 26, the top story on page 5 ",["revealed","https://www.roymorgan.com/findings/10341-voting-intentions-by-country-of-birth-september-2026"]," that Pickering left Victoria to join the Army while being pursued through the courts by the Commonwealth Bank for a debt of more than $200,000 after repossessing his house."]},{"k":"p","s":["The article noted Pickering was campaigning on a platform of radical transparency and had promised to “blow the books open” on the state’s finances."]},{"k":"p","s":["On November 24, The Age ",["published a story","https://www.theage.com.au/national/victoria/one-nation-candidate-spread-dezi-freeman-conspiracy-anti-muslim-rhetoric-20261123-p61gh5.html"]," saying One Nation’s candidate for Williamstown, Catherine Loughnan, had abused Muslims, claimed the murder of two police officers by the now-deceased Dezi Freeman was a social media conspiracy, and threatened civil war over COVID vaccinations."]},{"k":"p","s":["A week earlier, the paper had reported that One Nation’s general manager, Kelvin Morton, had gone on leave and might not return after claims he downplayed sexual assault and lied in his CV. The ",["article","https://www.theage.com.au/politics/federal/one-nation-in-turmoil-as-under-fire-party-manager-goes-on-leave-20261119-p61fpc.html"]," also referred to what it called the party’s chaotic internal processes."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is possible The Age might reconcile these glaring inconsistencies by reference to impartiality: covering the good side and the bad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Two arguments tell against that."]},{"k":"p","s":["First, if the bad is so bad, why promote the good at all?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Second, and more substantive, is that there are two classes of news here."]},{"k":"p","s":["The stories in the propaganda class are entirely discretionary. They exist because the paper had decided to call them into existence."]},{"k":"p","s":["The stories in the real news class are not discretionary at all. They have a strong public-interest element and were generated by events outside the newspaper’s control."]},{"k":"p","s":["The rise of One Nation is, in general terms, one of the two most salient features of the election. The other is that Labor is headed for a wipeout of historic proportions."]},{"k":"p","s":["But that does not explain the disorienting inconsistency."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Herald Sun and The Australian, by contrast, have known exactly what they want: the election of a Liberal-led government under Jess Wilson."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Australian has often found a place on page 1 for a pro-Liberal or anti-Labor story, and in particular ",["has explored","https://www.theaustralian.com.au/nation/politics/left-on-the-porch-luke-hilakari-attacks-ben-carroll-as-unions-pull-campaign-funds/news-story/08a8c7755694319d38fb3ae8103020e8"]," the political implications of Labor’s falling out with the union movement."]},{"k":"p","s":["Across the media, this is a very underdone story. A Labor premier calls a royal commission into union corruption on the Big Build and, because of this and other perceived weaknesses, is denied significant financial support for his campaign."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Herald Sun has produced a parade of Page 1 splashes: “Sick and sorry state”, “Jess soars as Ben bombs” and “Bye bye Ben”."]},{"k":"p","s":["All had overtones of jubilation, along with photos of Jess and her winning smile: Jess with her baby son, Jess serving at Maccas, Jess serving ice cream. And, as with the media generally, there has been little or no effort to hold her to account for her party’s policy positions."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is not going to matter this time, but it does our democracy no service when a major media organisation is just a cheerleader for one side of politics and there is insufficient scrutiny of a party which is almost certain to hold the reins of power after election day."]}],"chars":6340}};
